/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ROOT_STORIES batch 2 (shared Latin/Greek roots) + aliases
   ===================================================== */
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.alias, { pon: "pos", cap3: "cap", sent2: "sent", reg3: "reg", leg3: "leg", gen2: "gen", tra: "tract" });
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.shared, {
  thes: ["希腊语 tithēmi(放)的名词 thesis 本义\"放下的东西\"——一个立场、一个命题;在格律里 thesis 是脚\"放下\"的重拍。与拉丁语 facere、英语 do 同根(印欧语 *dʰeh₁-)。hypo-thesis 是放在底下的假设(亚里士多德的术语),syn-thesis 放在一起,anti-thesis 放在对面,paren-thesis 是\"放在旁边插进去\"的话(括号),pros-thesis 加上去的部件(假肢),epi-thet 加在名字上的称号,theme(thema)是放下来讨论的主题。treasure 和 thesaurus 也是它:thēsauros 是\"存放处\"——宝库,1852 年罗热用它命名词汇宝库。",
    "Greek tithēmi (to put) makes the noun thesis, \"a thing set down\" — a position, a proposition; in metre a thesis is the beat where the foot comes down. It shares PIE *dʰeh₁- with Latin facere and English do. A hypo-thesis is set underneath (Aristotle's term), a syn-thesis put together, an anti-thesis set opposite, a paren-thesis put in beside (brackets), a pros-thesis an added part (a limb), an epi-thet a name put on, a theme (thema) the subject set down for discussion. Treasure and thesaurus belong here too: a thēsauros is a place where things are put — a storehouse; Roget took it for his word-hoard in 1852."],
  itis: ["-itis 本是希腊语形容词阴性词尾,\"…的\";医学上它总是省略了名词 nosos(病):arthr-itis (nosos) 是\"关节的(病)\"。希波克拉底学派已用 nephritis(肾病)、pleuritis(胸膜病),但\"炎症\"这个专门义是罗马医生凯尔苏斯以后才固定的——他描述炎症的四大征象红、肿、热、痛(rubor, tumor, calor, dolor),至今医学院还在教。19 世纪细菌学兴起后,-itis 成了造词机器:bronch-itis(1808)、appendic-itis(1886,爱德华七世 1902 年加冕前因此手术而家喻户晓)。俗语 fiscalitis、telephonitis 是拿它开玩笑。",
    "-itis was simply a Greek feminine adjective ending, \"of, pertaining to\"; in medicine it always implies the noun nosos, disease: arthr-itis (nosos) is the disease of the joints. The Hippocratic writers already had nephritis and pleuritis, but the specific sense of inflammation settled after the Roman physician Celsus, who listed its four signs — rubor, tumor, calor, dolor (redness, swelling, heat, pain) — still taught in medical schools. With 19th-century bacteriology -itis became a word-factory: bronch-itis (1808), appendic-itis (1886, made famous when Edward VII was operated on before his 1902 coronation). Jokes like telephonitis play on it."],
  pop: ["populus(人民)是拉丁语借自伊特鲁里亚语的词,词源不明——它原指\"武装起来的公民全体\":罗马军队的口号 Senatus Populusque Romanus(元老院与罗马人民,SPQR)刻在每一面军旗上。popularis 是\"人民一边的\":共和末期的 populares 是平民派政治家(与贵族派 optimates 相对),popular 直到 17 世纪才有\"受欢迎\"的意思。publicus 是 populicus 的缩合——公共的(public、republic 是 res publica,公共事务),populatio 在古典拉丁语里竟是\"劫掠\"(把一地的人口掳走),18 世纪才转为\"人口\";populace、people(法语 peuple)、pueblo(西班牙语村镇)都是人民。",
    "populus (a people) is Latin borrowed from Etruscan, origin unknown — originally the citizen body under arms: the Roman army marched under Senatus Populusque Romanus, the Senate and People of Rome, SPQR on every standard. popularis meant \"of the people's side\": the populares of the late Republic were the popular politicians against the optimates; popular meant well-liked only from the 17th century. publicus is populicus contracted — public, and a republic is res publica, the people's business; populatio in classical Latin meant plundering (carrying off a district's people) and became \"population\" only in the 18th century; populace, people (French peuple) and Spanish pueblo (a village) are all the people."],
  in: ["拉丁语有两个不同的 in-。一个是介词 in(在里面、进入),与英语 in、希腊语 en 同根(印欧语 *h₁en):in-cludere 关在里面,in-vadere 进入,im-portare 运进来。另一个是否定前缀 in-,与英语 un-、希腊语 a(n)- 同根(印欧语 *n̥-):in-iustus 不义,im-possibilis 不可能。两个 in- 在辅音前都会同化:im- 在 p/b/m 前,il- 在 l 前,ir- 在 r 前——所以 illegal、irregular、immature 都是 in-。有时两者撞在一起:inflammable 是\"能燃烧\"(介词 in),却常被误读为\"不能燃烧\"——美国消防部门为此改用 flammable。invaluable 是\"无法估价\"(否定),而 inhabited 是\"有人住\"(介词)。",
    "Latin has two different in-. One is the preposition in (inside, into), cognate with English in and Greek en (PIE *h₁en): in-cludere to shut in, in-vadere to go in, im-portare to carry in. The other is the negative prefix in-, cognate with English un- and Greek a(n)- (PIE *n̥-): in-iustus unjust, im-possibilis impossible. Both assimilate before consonants — im- before p/b/m, il- before l, ir- before r — so illegal, irregular and immature are all in-. Sometimes they collide: inflammable means \"can catch fire\" (the preposition) but is often read as \"cannot\", which is why fire services switched to flammable; invaluable is beyond valuing (negative), while inhabited means lived in (preposition)."],
  rid: ["ridere(笑)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能是模拟笑声的拟声词。罗马喜剧作家普劳图斯的观众 rident;risus 是笑声,ridiculus 是\"会引人笑的\"——可笑(ridiculous),de-ridere 是朝下笑——嘲笑(deride、derision),ir-ridere 冲着人笑(irrision),sub-ridere 是\"底下笑\"——微笑,给了法语 sourire、意大利语 sorridere、西班牙语 sonreír;risible 可笑的,risqué 与此无关(是 risk)。中世纪修道院规则禁止 risus(大笑),只允许 subrisus——这就是《玫瑰之名》里那本失落的亚里士多德论喜剧的书为什么危险。",
    "ridere (to laugh) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin — perhaps an imitation of laughter itself. The audiences of Plautus rident; risus is laughter, ridiculus what makes people laugh — ridiculous, de-ridere laughing down at — deride (derision), ir-ridere laughing at someone (irrision), sub-ridere laughing underneath — to smile, which gave French sourire, Italian sorridere, Spanish sonreír; risible is laughable (risqué is unrelated, from risk). Monastic rules forbade risus, loud laughter, and allowed only subrisus — which is why the lost book of Aristotle on comedy is so dangerous in The Name of the Rose."],
  sign: ["signum(记号、军旗、雕像)可能来自印欧语 *sekʷ-(跟随)——记号是让人跟着走的东西,与 sequi 同根。罗马军团每个中队都有 signum(军旗),signifer 是旗手;signare 是盖印、做记号,signum 也指印章戒指上的图案(sign、seal 都由此而来:sigillum 小印章 → seal)。de-signare 是画出记号指定(designate、design),as-signare 分派(assign),con-signare 盖印交付(consign),re-signare 解封——放弃(resign),signi-ficare 做出记号——表示(signify、significant),in-signis 有标记的——杰出(insignia 徽章),signal 是法语的信号,signature 签名,sign 在 14 世纪才从记号变成手势。",
    "signum (a mark, standard, statue) may come from PIE *sekʷ- (to follow) — a sign is what one follows, kin to sequi. Every maniple of a Roman legion carried a signum, its standard, borne by the signifer; signare was to stamp, to mark, and signum was also the device on a seal-ring (both sign and seal descend from it: sigillum, little signum, became seal). de-signare marks out and appoints (designate, design), as-signare allots (assign), con-signare seals and delivers (consign), re-signare unseals — gives up (resign), signi-ficare makes a sign — means (signify, significant), in-signis marked out — distinguished (insignia); a signal is French, a signature a signing, and sign became a gesture only in the 14th century."],
  spir: ["spirare(呼吸、吹)是拉丁语独有的拟声词。spiritus 先是\"一口气\"——风、呼吸,西塞罗时代引申为\"精神\";基督教用 Spiritus Sanctus 译希腊语 pneuma hagion(圣灵),spirit 因此有了灵魂义。re-spirare 反复呼吸(respiration),ex-spirare 呼出最后一口气——死(expire,后指到期),in-spirare 吹进去——神把灵感吹进诗人(inspire),con-spirare 一起呼吸——密谋(conspire),a-spirare 朝着呼吸——渴望(aspire),per-spirare 透过皮肤呼吸——出汗(perspire),su-spirare 从底下叹息(法语 soupirer)。spirit 蒸馏出的酒精也是\"气\",sprite 精灵是 spiritus 经法语 esprit 的磨损。",
    "spirare (to breathe, blow) is an onomatopoeic word found only in Latin. spiritus was first a breath — wind, breathing — and by Cicero's time also the spirit; the Church used Spiritus Sanctus for Greek pneuma hagion, the Holy Spirit, which gave spirit its soul-sense. re-spirare is breathing again and again (respiration), ex-spirare breathing out the last breath — to die (expire, later of contracts), in-spirare breathing in — the god breathing into the poet (inspire), con-spirare breathing together — plotting (conspire), a-spirare breathing toward — longing (aspire), per-spirare breathing through the skin — sweating (perspire), su-spirare sighing from below (French soupirer). Distilled spirits are \"breath\" of wine; a sprite is spiritus worn down through French esprit."],
  lig: ["ligare(绑)来自印欧语 *leig-(绑),同根词很少:阿尔巴尼亚语 lidh(绑)。罗马人 ligare 伤口(ligature 绷带、结扎),ob-ligare 是\"绑住某人\"——立下义务(obligation;oblige 本义使人受约束,后来才有\"帮忙\"义),re-ligare 再绑——西塞罗以外的古人(拉克坦提乌斯)认为 religio 是\"把人重新绑向神\",al-ligare 绑上(alloy 合金是绑在一起的金属,ally 盟友是绑在一起的人),col-ligare 绑成捆(colleague 与此无关,那是 legare 委派),liga 是同盟(league),liable 是法语\"可被绑的\"——负有责任,rely 是 re-ligare 经法语——把自己绑上去依靠。lien 是法律上的\"绑定\"——留置权。",
    "ligare (to bind) comes from PIE *leig- (to bind), with few cognates — Albanian lidh (to tie). Romans ligare a wound (a ligature is a bandage or a tying-off), ob-ligare binds a person — an obligation (to oblige first meant to bind someone, only later to do a favour), re-ligare binds again — Lactantius, unlike Cicero, derived religio from man being bound back to God, al-ligare binds to (an alloy is metals bound together, an ally a person bound to you), col-ligare binds in a bundle (colleague is unrelated: legare, to depute), liga is a league, liable is French \"bindable\" — answerable, rely is re-ligare through French — binding yourself to something. A lien is a legal binding — a right to hold."],
  spond: ["spondere(郑重许诺)来自印欧语 *spend-(奠酒、立约),同根的希腊语 spendō 是\"奠酒\"、spondē 是奠酒立下的休战约。罗马订婚就是 sponsalia:父亲问\"你答应把女儿给我儿子吗?\"——Spondesne? Spondeo(我答应)。所以 sponsus 是新郎、sponsa 是新娘(法语 époux/épouse,英语 spouse),sponsor 是担保人(教父母,后来指赞助商),re-spondere 是\"回答一个许诺\"——回应(respond、response、responsible 是能担保的),cor-respondere 互相回应(correspond),de-spondere 是许出去、后来指\"放弃希望\"(despond 沮丧),spontaneus 是\"出于自己的许诺\"——自发的(spontaneous)。",
    "spondere (to pledge solemnly) comes from PIE *spend- (to pour a libation, make a pact); Greek spendō is to pour a libation and spondē the truce sealed by one. A Roman betrothal was the sponsalia: the father was asked Spondesne? — do you pledge your daughter to my son? — and answered Spondeo. So sponsus is the bridegroom, sponsa the bride (French époux/épouse, English spouse), a sponsor a guarantor (a godparent, later a paying backer), re-spondere to answer a pledge — respond (response; responsible means able to answer for), cor-respondere to answer each other (correspond), de-spondere to pledge away, later to give up hope (despond), spontaneus of one's own pledge — spontaneous."],
  string: ["stringere(拉紧、扎紧)来自印欧语 *streig-(擦、勒),同根的英语词是 strike、streak、德语 Strick(绳)。罗马人 stringere 剑是\"拔剑\"(从鞘里勒出来),stringere 也指勒紧腰带、勒紧钱袋。分词 strictus 是勒紧的——严格(strict),con-stringere 一起勒紧——约束(constrain、constrict),re-stringere 勒回去——限制(restrict、restrain),di-stringere 拉向两边——分心(distract 的近亲 distrain、district 是被\"管束\"的区域),a-stringens 收敛的(astringent),prae-stringere 勒得发晕——prestige 本义\"迷惑的把戏\"(法语 prestige,19 世纪才转为威望)。stress 和 strain 都是 stringere 经法语的形状。",
    "stringere (to draw tight, bind) comes from PIE *streig- (to stroke, press), the root of English strike and streak and German Strick (a rope). Romans stringere a sword — drew it, pulling it tight out of the sheath — and stringere a belt or a purse. The participle strictus is drawn tight — strict; con-stringere binds together (constrain, constrict), re-stringere binds back (restrict, restrain), di-stringere pulls apart (distrain; a district was land held under constraint), a-stringens contracts (astringent), prae-stringere binds so tight the eyes blur — prestige first meant a dazzling trick (French prestige) and only in the 19th century standing. Stress and strain are stringere in French shapes."],
  plan: ["planus(平的)来自印欧语 *pleh₂-(平、宽),同根有英语 floor、field、德语 Feld、希腊语 platys(宽,plat- 是它的另一条线)。罗马人说 planities 平原(plain),planus 也指\"清楚明白\"(plain 明白、explain 是 ex-planare 把褶皱摊平),plana 是刨子(plane)。plan(计划)是 17 世纪法语从 plan(平面图)来的——建筑师先画平面,piano(钢琴)是意大利语 piano e forte(轻与响)的缩写,piano 又来自 planus 的 pianus(平、轻),plateau、plaza、piazza、place 都是 platea(希腊语 plateia hodos,宽街)——广场;planet 与此无关(希腊语 planētēs,漫游者)。",
    "planus (flat) comes from PIE *pleh₂- (flat, broad), the root of English floor and field, German Feld, Greek platys (broad; plat- is its other line). Romans said planities for a plain, and planus also meant clear (plain, and explain is ex-planare, smoothing out the folds); a plana is a plane for smoothing wood. Plan came from 17th-century French plan, a ground-plan — the architect draws the flat first; piano is short for Italian piano e forte (soft and loud), piano itself from planus via pianus (level, gentle); plateau, plaza, piazza and place are all platea (Greek plateia hodos, a broad street) — the square. Planet is unrelated (Greek planētēs, a wanderer)."],
  solid: ["solidus(整块的、坚实的)来自印欧语 *solh₂-(完整),同根有 salvus(完好)、sollus(全部)、希腊语 holos(整,holo-)、英语 whole 经不同路径。罗马人说 solidus 的金子是不掺假的整块金;君士坦丁 309 年发行的金币就叫 solidus——它稳定了七百年,成为中世纪的标准货币,名字磨损成法语 sou、意大利语 soldo、西班牙语 sueldo(工资!),soldier(士兵)是 soldarius:领 solidus 军饷的人。con-solidare 合成一块(consolidate),solidaritas 是 19 世纪法国工人运动从 solidum(共同责任)造出的\"团结\"(solidarity),solder(焊)是把金属熔成一块,sold 与此无关。",
    "solidus (all of a piece, firm) comes from PIE *solh₂- (whole), shared with salvus (safe), sollus (entire), Greek holos (holo-) and, by another route, English whole. Roman solidus gold was unalloyed, one solid mass; the gold coin Constantine issued in 309 was called the solidus — it held its value for seven centuries, became the standard coin of the Middle Ages, and wore down into French sou, Italian soldo, Spanish sueldo (wages!). A soldier is a soldarius, one paid in solidi. con-solidare makes one mass (consolidate), solidaritas was coined by 19th-century French workers from solidum, joint liability (solidarity), solder fuses metal into one piece; sold is unrelated."],
  para: ["希腊语 para(在旁边、越过)来自印欧语 *per-(穿过、在前),与拉丁语 per、pro 和英语 for 同根。在希腊语里它既表示\"旁边\"(para-llēlos 并排的线,para-graphos 是抄本页边的一道线,标出段落),也表示\"偏离、反常\"(para-doxos 违反常识,para-noia 心智偏离,para-sitos 是\"在别人饭桌旁吃\"的食客)。医学 para-lysis 是\"旁边松了\"——半身不遂。英语的 para- 还有第三个来源:意大利语 para-(挡,来自 parare):para-chute 挡下落,para-sol 挡太阳,para-pet 挡胸——与希腊语无关。",
    "Greek para (beside, beyond) comes from PIE *per- (through, before), cognate with Latin per and pro and English for. In Greek it means both \"alongside\" (para-llēlos lines side by side, para-graphos the stroke in a manuscript margin marking a new section) and \"aside, contrary\" (para-doxos against expectation, para-noia a mind gone aside, para-sitos one who eats beside another's table). Medical para-lysis is a loosening on one side. English para- has a third source, Italian para- (to ward off, from parare): para-chute stops a fall, para-sol wards off sun, para-pet guards the chest — nothing Greek about them."],
  mater: ["mater(母)是印欧语 *méh₂tēr——ma 是婴儿最先发出的音,-ter 是亲属后缀(与 pater、frater 同构):梵语 mātar、希腊语 mētēr、英语 mother、德语 Mutter、俄语 mat'。罗马人把它推广到一切\"生出东西的源头\":materia 是树的\"母干\"、木料,后来泛指材料(material、matter——物质就是母亲),matrix 是母兽的子宫,后来指模具、数学矩阵,matri-monium 是\"成为母亲的状态\"——婚姻(所以 matrimony 只从女方角度命名),metropolis 是希腊语的\"母城\"(殖民地的宗主城),alma mater 是\"哺育的母亲\"——古罗马对谷神刻瑞斯的称呼,18 世纪才用于母校,matrona 已婚妇女(matron),maternity 母性。",
    "mater (mother) is Indo-European *méh₂tēr — ma, the baby's first sound, plus the kinship suffix -ter (as in pater, frater): Sanskrit mātar, Greek mētēr, English mother, German Mutter, Russian mat'. Romans extended it to any source that brings things forth: materia was the tree's mother-trunk, timber, then material in general (material, matter — matter is mother), matrix the womb of a breeding animal, then a mould and a mathematical matrix, matri-monium the state of becoming a mother — marriage (so matrimony is named from the wife's side only), metropolis Greek for the mother-city of a colony, alma mater the nourishing mother — Rome's title for Ceres, applied to universities from the 18th century, matrona a married woman (matron), maternity motherhood."],
  mod: ["modus(尺度、方式)来自印欧语 *med-(量、思量),同根有 mederi(治疗,量病开药)、meditari(思量)、英语 mete(量)、德语 messen。罗马人的 modus 首先是量:modius 是量谷物的斗;然后是节制——ne quid nimis(凡事勿过),modestus 守分寸的(modest),moderari 保持在尺度内(moderate),modicus 适度的;然后是方式:modo 是\"刚刚、只是\",modernus 是晚期拉丁语\"当下的\"(modern,来自 modo\"刚才\"),modulus 是小尺度——建筑的基本尺寸(module),modulari 按尺度调节(modulate),modellus 是意大利语 modello(模型),mode 是方式、又是时装(法语 mode → 德语 Mode),mood 在语法里是 modus 的英语形状。commodity 是\"合尺度的\"——便利,后来指商品。",
    "modus (measure, manner) comes from PIE *med- (to measure, take counsel), shared with mederi (to heal — measuring out a remedy), meditari (to ponder), English mete and German messen. Roman modus was first a measure — the modius was the grain-bushel; then moderation, ne quid nimis, nothing too much: modestus keeps within bounds (modest), moderari holds to the measure (moderate), modicus is moderate; then manner: modo means \"just now, only\", and late Latin modernus (from modo) means of the present — modern; modulus is a small measure, the basic unit of a building (module), modulari to adjust by measure (modulate), Italian modello a model, mode a manner and a fashion (French mode, German Mode), and mood in grammar is modus in English dress. A commodity was what fits the measure — a convenience, then goods."],
  cult: ["colere(耕种、居住、敬拜)来自印欧语 *kʷel-(转、绕),同根的希腊语是 polos(轴,pole 极)、telos(转到头——终点)、英语 wheel。罗马人\"绕着\"一块地转就是耕种它,绕着神转就是敬拜它:cultus 既是耕作也是崇拜(cult),cultura 先是农业(agri-cultura),西塞罗第一个说 cultura animi(心灵的耕耘)——culture 由此指文明教养,colonus 是耕作者,colonia 是他们被安置的地(colony;科隆 Köln 就是 Colonia Agrippina),in-cola 居民,cultivate 耕种、培养,couture 与此无关。\"文化\"是耕出来的,不是天生的——这个隐喻从西塞罗一直用到今天。",
    "colere (to till, dwell, worship) comes from PIE *kʷel- (to turn, go round), the root of Greek polos (axis, pole), telos (the turn's end — a goal) and English wheel. To go round a plot of land was to farm it; to go round a god was to worship him: cultus is both tillage and cult, cultura was first farming (agri-cultura) until Cicero spoke of cultura animi, the tillage of the mind — whence culture as civilised refinement; a colonus is a farmer, a colonia the land they were settled on (colony; Cologne is Colonia Agrippina), an in-cola an inhabitant, cultivate to till or to nurture; couture is unrelated. Culture is grown, not born — a metaphor in use from Cicero to now."],
  grat: ["gratus(受欢迎的、感激的)来自印欧语 *gʷerH-(赞美、欢迎),同根有梵语 gṛṇāti(赞美)、立陶宛语 giriù。罗马人的 gratia 是\"讨人喜欢\"——恩惠、也是感激:gratias agere(表达感谢)是西班牙语 gracias、意大利语 grazie 的直接来源;基督教用 gratia 译希腊语 charis(神的白白恩典,grace);gratis 是 gratiis 的缩合——\"仅凭恩惠\"、不要钱;grati-ficare 使人高兴(gratify),con-gratulari 一起道喜(congratulate),in-gratus 忘恩的,gratuitus 白给的——gratuity 是小费(白给的钱),也是\"无端的\"(gratuitous),agree 是 a-gratare(合心意),grateful 是 gratus 加英语 -ful。三美神 Gratiae 就是希腊的 Charites。",
    "gratus (welcome, thankful) comes from PIE *gʷerH- (to praise, welcome), shared with Sanskrit gṛṇāti (praises) and Lithuanian giriù. Roman gratia was pleasingness — a favour, and thanks: gratias agere, to give thanks, is the direct source of Spanish gracias and Italian grazie; the Church used gratia for Greek charis, God's free gift — grace; gratis is gratiis contracted — \"by favour alone\", for nothing; grati-ficare pleases (gratify), con-gratulari rejoices together (congratulate), in-gratus is unthankful, gratuitus given free — a gratuity is money freely given, and gratuitous means unwarranted; agree is a-gratare, to come to one's liking; grateful is gratus plus English -ful. The three Graces, Gratiae, are the Greek Charites."],
  clar: ["clarus(明亮、响亮、著名)来自印欧语 *kelh₁-(喊),同根有 clamare(喊)、calendae(被\"喊出\"的每月初一)、希腊语 kalein(叫)、英语 low(牛叫)——clarus 最初是声音的\"响亮\",后来才指光的明亮和名声的显赫。罗马人称显贵为 vir clarissimus(最著名的人),4 世纪后成了元老阶级的正式称号。clari-ficare 使清楚(clarify),de-clarare 讲明白——宣告(declare),clarus 的法语形状 clair 给了 clear、clairvoyant(看得清的);clarion 是响亮的号,clarinet 是\"小号角\"(意大利语 clarinetto),claret 是法语 vin clairet(清亮的淡红酒),Clara、Clarence 都是\"光明\"。",
    "clarus (bright, loud, famous) comes from PIE *kelh₁- (to shout), shared with clamare (to cry out), calendae (the first of the month, \"called out\"), Greek kalein (to call) and English low (of cattle) — clarus was first loudness of sound, then brightness of light and of reputation. A Roman grandee was vir clarissimus, most famous man, from the 4th century the formal title of senators. clari-ficare makes clear (clarify), de-clarare makes plain — declares; the French form clair gave clear and clairvoyant (clear-seeing); a clarion is a loud trumpet, a clarinet Italian clarinetto, a little clarion, claret French vin clairet, clear pale-red wine, and Clara and Clarence both mean bright."],
  gel: ["gelu(霜、冰)来自印欧语 *gel-(冷),同根有英语 cold、cool、chill、德语 kalt、希腊语 gelandron(寒冷)。罗马人 gelare 是结冻,con-gelare 冻成一块(congeal),gelidus 冰冷的(gelid),gelatina 是意大利语\"冻住的东西\"——肉冻(gelatine),gelato 就是\"冻的\"——冰淇淋,jelly 是 gelata 经法语 gelée;glacies(冰)是同根的另一形式,给了 glacier(冰川)、glacial、glass 与此无关。法语 gel 是霜冻,dégeler 解冻。北方日耳曼人的 cold 和南方罗马人的 gelu,是同一个古老的\"冷\"。",
    "gelu (frost, ice) comes from PIE *gel- (cold), the root of English cold, cool and chill, German kalt, Greek gelandron (chilly). Romans gelare, to freeze, con-gelare freeze into one mass (congeal), gelidus icy (gelid); gelatina is Italian for \"frozen stuff\" — meat jelly (gelatine), gelato simply \"frozen\" — ice cream, jelly is gelata via French gelée; glacies (ice) is a sister form that gave glacier and glacial (glass is unrelated). French gel is frost, dégeler to thaw. The Germanic north's cold and the Roman south's gelu are the same ancient chill."],
  quadr: ["quattuor(四)是印欧语 *kʷetwóres:希腊语 tettares/tessares、梵语 catvāras、英语 four、德语 vier、俄语 chetyre。拉丁语的组合形式 quadr- 造了 quadratus 方的(square 是 ex-quadra 经法语磨损,quadrant 四分之一圆,quadrangle 四边形),quartus 第四(quarter 四分之一;quart 是四分之一加仑;quarters 军营是城的\"一个区\"),quadraginta 四十(Lent 的法语 carême 是 quadragesima,大斋期四十天),quadrivium 中世纪大学的\"四艺\"(算术、几何、音乐、天文),squad 是意大利语 squadra(方阵)——squadron 是方阵队,quarantine 是威尼斯的 quaranta giorni——入港船只隔离四十天(1377 年拉古萨首创)。",
    "quattuor (four) is Indo-European *kʷetwóres: Greek tettares/tessares, Sanskrit catvāras, English four, German vier, Russian chetyre. Latin's combining form quadr- built quadratus, squared (square is ex-quadra worn down by French; quadrant, quadrangle), quartus fourth (quarter; a quart is a quarter gallon; quarters are a city's district), quadraginta forty (French carême, Lent, is quadragesima, the forty days), the quadrivium, the medieval university's four arts (arithmetic, geometry, music, astronomy), squad from Italian squadra, a square formation (squadron), and quarantine from Venetian quaranta giorni — forty days' isolation for incoming ships, first imposed at Ragusa in 1377."],
  tal: ["拉丁语有两个 tal-。一是 talis(如此的),来自印欧语指示词 *to-(与 the、that 同根):qualis…talis(什么样…就那样)给了 quality 和 tally 无关的 talis→意大利语 tale。二是晚期拉丁语 taliare(切),来自 talea(插条、切下的枝条):法语 tailler 切、裁——tailor 裁缝,tally 是刻痕计数的木棍(英国财政部用到 1826 年,烧旧木棍时把议会大厦烧了),detail 是切下来的小块(细节),retail 是切成小份卖(零售),entail 是切定继承顺序,tallage 是切下的税,taille 法国旧王朝的人头税。所以 detail、retail、tailor 都是\"切\"。",
    "Latin has two tal-. One is talis (such), from the demonstrative *to- (cognate with the and that): qualis…talis, of what kind…of that kind, gave quality and Italian tale. The other is late Latin taliare (to cut), from talea (a cutting, a slip of wood): French tailler to cut, to shape — a tailor cuts cloth, a tally was a notched stick for counting (used by the English Exchequer until 1826; burning the old sticks set Parliament on fire), a detail is a piece cut off, retail is selling in cut portions, to entail is to cut the line of inheritance, tallage a tax cut out, taille the old French poll tax. Detail, retail and tailor are all cutting."],
  var: ["varius(斑驳的、多样的)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能与 varus(弯曲的、罗圈腿)同源。它先指颜色:varius 的皮毛是有花斑的(vair 是中世纪纹章学里的灰白花斑毛皮——灰姓灰姑娘的\"玻璃鞋\"很可能是 pantoufle de vair 毛皮鞋的误听);然后指多样:varietas 多样(variety),variare 改变(vary),varia 杂集(variorum 是\"各家注释本\"),variabilis 可变的(variable),variatio 变奏(variation)。varicose(静脉曲张)来自 varix,弯曲的血管,与 varus 同根。",
    "varius (speckled, diverse) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin — perhaps akin to varus (bent, bow-legged). It began with colour: a varius hide is dappled (vair is the grey-and-white squirrel fur of medieval heraldry — Cinderella's glass slipper is probably a mishearing of pantoufle de vair, a fur slipper); then with diversity: varietas (variety), variare (to vary), varia miscellanies (a variorum edition gathers various commentators), variabilis (variable), variatio (variation). Varicose comes from varix, a twisted vein, kin to varus."],
  quir: ["quaerere(寻找、询问)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马法官 quaerere 是审讯,quaestio 是法庭调查(question 先是审问,后来才是问题),quaestor 是查账的财务官——共和国最低一级的官职。在复合词里变 -quirere/-quisitum:in-quirere 追问(inquire、inquest、inquisition——宗教裁判所就是\"调查所\"),re-quirere 反复找——需要(require、request、requisition),ac-quirere 找到手(acquire),ex-quisitus 精心找出的——精美(exquisite),con-quirere 搜集,conquest 是法语 conquérir(追求并夺取),quest 是骑士的寻找,query 是 quaere(\"你去查\"),questionnaire 是 19 世纪法语。",
    "quaerere (to seek, ask) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. A Roman judge quaerere held an inquiry; a quaestio was a judicial investigation (a question was an interrogation before it was a query), a quaestor the auditing treasurer — the Republic's lowest magistracy. In compounds it becomes -quirere/-quisitum: in-quirere to ask into (inquire, inquest, inquisition — the Holy Office was simply the \"investigation\"), re-quirere to keep seeking — to need (require, request, requisition), ac-quirere to get by seeking (acquire), ex-quisitus sought out with care — exquisite, con-quirere to gather, conquest French conquérir, to seek and win, a quest a knight's search, query the imperative quaere, \"go and ask\", questionnaire 19th-century French."],
  gest: ["gerere(携带、进行、做)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人 gerere 是\"身上带着\"——穿衣、带武器,引申为\"进行\"战争、\"担任\"官职:res gestae 是\"做过的事\"——功业(奥古斯都的自传就叫《Res Gestae》)。分词 gestus 是姿态——身体\"带\"出的动作(gesture、gesticulate),con-gerere 堆在一起(congest 堵塞),di-gerere 分开带走——消化(digest;文摘 digest 是把材料分门别类),sug-gerere 从下面带上来——暗示(suggest),ex-aggerare 堆高(exaggerate),re-gistrum 是 regesta(带回来记下的东西)——登记册(register),gestatio 是怀孕(gestation,\"带着胎儿\"),belli-gerent 是\"进行战争的\"。",
    "gerere (to carry, conduct, do) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Romans gerere meant to carry on the body — clothes, weapons — and so to carry on a war or bear an office: res gestae are things done — deeds (Augustus' autobiography is the Res Gestae). The participle gestus is a bearing, the movement the body carries out (gesture, gesticulate); con-gerere heaps together (congest), di-gerere carries apart — digests (a digest sorts material into heads), sug-gerere brings up from beneath — suggests, ex-aggerare piles high (exaggerate), re-gistrum is regesta, things carried back and recorded — a register, gestatio is pregnancy (gestation, carrying the child), belli-gerent is waging war."],
  oper: ["opus(作品,属格 operis)来自印欧语 *h₃ep-(工作、产出),同根有梵语 apas(工作)、古英语 efnan(做);ops(财富、力量)是同一个根——工作产出财富,女神 Ops 是丰饶之神,Opulentia 富裕。罗马人的 opera 是 opus 的复数——\"许多劳作\"、也指付出的工夫,意大利语 opera 就是\"作品\",1600 年前后专指歌剧;operari 做工(operate;手术 operation 是医生的\"操作\"),co-operari 一起做,opi-fex 工匠,opus 的属格给了 opera-tor、opus magnum(炼金术的伟大工作);office 是 opi-ficium(做事)——职责、办公处,official 官员,officer 军官。opulent 富有,optimus 与此无关(ops 的最高级说法有争议)。",
    "opus (a work, genitive operis) comes from PIE *h₃ep- (to work, produce), shared with Sanskrit apas (work) and Old English efnan (to perform); ops (wealth, power) is the same root — work yields wealth, the goddess Ops is plenty. Roman opera is the plural of opus — many labours, and also effort spent; Italian opera is simply \"a work\", and around 1600 came to mean the sung drama; operari is to work (operate; a surgeon's operation is his working), co-operari to work together, an opi-fex a craftsman; office is opi-ficium, the doing of a duty — a post, a workplace, official and officer follow. Opulent is rich in ops; whether optimus belongs here is disputed."],
  rot: ["rota(轮)来自印欧语 *Hret-(滚、跑),同根有梵语 ratha(战车)、古爱尔兰语 roth、德语 Rad(轮)、立陶宛语 ratas。罗马人 rotare 是转轮,rotundus 是\"轮形的\"——圆(round 是 rotundus 经法语,rotunda 圆厅),rotula 小轮——膝盖骨(patella 的别名),rotatio 旋转(rotation),rota 在中世纪教会法里是轮流审案的法庭(Rota Romana 至今是教廷最高法院),rotulus 卷轴——roll(卷、名册、面包卷),control 是 contra-rotulus(核对副本的卷册),enrol 写进名册,role 是演员台词的卷子(法语 rôle),rodeo 是西班牙语 rodear(绕圈赶牛),roulette 是法语小轮。",
    "rota (a wheel) comes from PIE *Hret- (to roll, run), shared with Sanskrit ratha (chariot), Old Irish roth, German Rad (wheel), Lithuanian ratas. Romans rotare turned a wheel; rotundus is wheel-shaped — round (round is rotundus through French; a rotunda a round hall), rotula a little wheel — the kneecap, rotatio turning (rotation); in canon law the rota was a court that heard cases in rotation (the Rota Romana is still the Church's high court); rotulus is a scroll — a roll (of parchment, of names, of bread), control is contra-rotulus, the duplicate roll checked against the original, to enrol is to write on the roll, a role is the actor's scroll of lines (French rôle), rodeo is Spanish rodear, to round up cattle, roulette a French little wheel."],
  ut: ["uti(用)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明;分词 usus 给了这一族。罗马法把 usus 定义为\"使用权\"——一年连续 usus 就能取得对某物的所有权(usucapio);usus fructus(使用收益权,usufruct)至今是民法术语。utilis 有用的(utility 效用,utensil 用具),usura 是\"使用金钱的代价\"——利息,中世纪教会禁止,所以 usury 成了贬义的高利贷;usurpare 是\"用手抓取来用\"——僭取(usurp),usualis 惯常的(usual),ab-uti 用过头(abuse),utensilia 用具。use 是 uti 经法语 user,peruse 是\"用完\"——细读,utilitarian 功利主义是 1780 年代边沁的自造词。",
    "uti (to use) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin; its participle usus supplies the family. Roman law defined usus as a right of use — a year's unbroken usus gave ownership (usucapio); usus fructus, the right to use and take the fruits (usufruct), is still a civil-law term. utilis is useful (utility, utensil), usura the price of using money — interest, which the medieval Church forbade, so usury became a word of blame; usurpare is to seize for one's use (usurp), usualis customary (usual), ab-uti to use up, misuse (abuse), utensilia implements. Use is uti through French user, peruse is to use up — to read through, utilitarian Bentham's coinage of the 1780s."],
  ang: ["拉丁语有三个纠缠的 ang-。angulus(角)来自印欧语 *ang-/*ank-(弯),同根有英语 ankle、angle(先指鱼钩)、希腊语 ankylos(弯的);angere(勒紧、使痛苦)来自 *angʰ-(窄、勒),同根有德语 eng(窄)、Angst(恐惧)、英语 anger(古挪威语 angr,悲伤)、希腊语 ankhein(勒死)——angina 是胸口被勒紧的痛,anxius 是心被勒着的——焦虑(anxiety、anguish 是 angustia 狭窄经法语)。angustus 窄的。第三个 angelus 是希腊语 angelos(信使)——天使,与前两者无关。angle 与 anxiety 的共同点是\"弯到挤\"。",
    "Three tangled ang- live in Latin. angulus (a corner) comes from PIE *ang-/*ank- (to bend), shared with English ankle and angle (first a fish-hook) and Greek ankylos (bent); angere (to squeeze, torment) comes from *angʰ- (narrow, choke), shared with German eng (narrow) and Angst, English anger (Old Norse angr, grief), Greek ankhein (to strangle) — angina is the chest squeezed with pain, anxius the heart squeezed — anxiety, anguish (angustia, straits, through French), angustus narrow. The third, angelus, is Greek angelos, a messenger — an angel, unrelated to both. What angle and anxiety share is bending until it pinches."],
  arm: ["arma(武器)与 armus(肩、上臂)同根:印欧语 *h₂er-(接合、装配),同根有英语 arm(手臂)、希腊语 harmos(接缝,harmony 是\"接合得好\")、arthron(关节,arthritis)、拉丁语 ars(技艺——把东西接合起来的本领)。arma 在拉丁语只有复数——\"装备\"是一套;armare 武装(army 是 armata,武装起来的队伍,armada 是西班牙语的同一个词,1588 年的无敌舰队),armatura 是护甲(armour、armature),armilla 臂环,armarium 是放武器的柜子——后来放什么都行(armoire、意大利语 armadio 衣柜),armistice 武器站住,alarm 是意大利语 all'arme!(拿起武器!),gendarme 是 gens d'armes(武装的人)。",
    "arma (weapons) and armus (shoulder, upper arm) share a root: PIE *h₂er- (to fit, join together), which also gave English arm, Greek harmos (a joint; harmony is good fitting), arthron (a joint; arthritis) and Latin ars (skill — the craft of joining things). arma is plural-only in Latin — gear comes as a set; armare is to arm (an army is an armata, a body armed; armada is the same word in Spanish, the fleet of 1588), armatura is protective gear (armour, armature), armilla an arm-ring, armarium a cupboard for weapons — later for anything (French armoire, Italian armadio, a wardrobe), armistice a standing-still of arms, alarm Italian all'arme! — to arms!, gendarme gens d'armes, men-at-arms."],
  sper: ["spes(希望)来自印欧语 *speh₁-(兴旺、成功),同根有英语 speed(古义\"成功、顺利\"——God speed 是\"愿神使你顺利\")、德语 spät(晚)的远亲有争议。罗马人 sperare 是希望,de-sperare 失去希望(despair、desperate 是绝望到不顾一切的),pro-sperus 是\"合乎希望的\"——顺利(prosper、prosperity),spes 本身没有传下来,但 Esperanza(西班牙语)、Esperanto(1887 年柴门霍夫的笔名\"怀着希望的人\")、法语 espoir、意大利语 speranza 都还在希望。",
    "spes (hope) comes from PIE *speh₁- (to thrive, succeed), the root of English speed (originally success — God speed means may God prosper you). Romans sperare to hope, de-sperare to lose hope (despair; the desperate are hopeless enough to try anything), pro-sperus \"according to hope\" — favourable (prosper, prosperity). spes itself did not survive, but Spanish Esperanza, Esperanto (Zamenhof's 1887 pen-name, \"one who hopes\"), French espoir and Italian speranza are still hoping."],
  aut: ["希腊语 autos(自己、同一个)词源不明,可能是 au(再)+ to(那个)的合成。它是希腊语最能造词的前缀之一:auto-nomos 自己立法的城邦(autonomy——希腊城邦最珍视的东西),auto-kratōr 自己掌权的(autocrat;拜占庭皇帝的正式头衔),auto-matos 自己动的(automaton;亚里士多德讨论过自动机),auth-entēs 是亲手做的人——authentic 本义\"作者亲笔的\",auto-graphos 亲笔,auto-psia 亲眼看——尸检(autopsy),auto-mobile 是 1876 年法语希腊拉丁混造的\"自己动的车\",autism 是 1911 年布洛伊勒造的\"自我封闭\"。auto 在意大利语、德语里就是汽车。",
    "Greek autos (self, the same) is of uncertain origin, perhaps au (again) + to (that). It is among the most productive Greek prefixes: auto-nomos a city that makes its own laws (autonomy — what Greek cities prized most), auto-kratōr ruling by oneself (autocrat; the Byzantine emperor's formal title), auto-matos self-moving (automaton; Aristotle discussed them), auth-entēs one who acts with his own hand — authentic meant in the author's own hand, auto-graphos own writing, auto-psia seeing for oneself — an autopsy, automobile an 1876 French Greek-Latin hybrid, \"self-moving\", autism Bleuler's 1911 coinage for withdrawal into the self. In Italian and German an auto is simply a car."],
  fid: ["fides(信任、信义)来自印欧语 *bʰeidʰ-(信赖、说服),同根有希腊语 peithō(说服)、pistis(信)、英语 bide(等待)、bid。fides 是罗马人最看重的德性:Fides 女神有神庙,签约时握右手就是把 fides 交给对方;bona fides(善意)至今是法律术语。fidelis 忠诚的(fidelity;狗名 Fido 是\"我信任\"),con-fidere 完全信任(confide、confidence;confidant 是被托付秘密的人),dif-fidere 不信任——羞怯(diffident),per-fidus 是\"越过信义\"——背信(perfidious),federe 是结盟——foedus 条约(federal、confederate 都是靠 fides 绑在一起的),affiance 订婚是法语的\"托付信任\",fiancé 就是被托付的人,defy 本义\"撤回信任\"——挑战。faith 是 fides 经法语 feid。",
    "fides (trust, good faith) comes from PIE *bʰeidʰ- (to trust, persuade), shared with Greek peithō (to persuade) and pistis (faith), English bide and bid. fides was the virtue Romans prized most: the goddess Fides had a temple, and clasping right hands at a contract handed one's fides to the other party; bona fides is still a legal term. fidelis is loyal (fidelity; the dog's name Fido is \"I trust\"), con-fidere to trust fully (confide, confidence; a confidant is entrusted with secrets), dif-fidere to distrust — shy (diffident), per-fidus beyond faith — treacherous (perfidious); foedus is a treaty, an alliance bound by fides (federal, confederate), affiance is French \"to entrust\", a fiancé the entrusted one, defy meant to withdraw one's trust — to challenge. Faith is fides in its French form feid."],
  rupt: ["rumpere(打破)来自印欧语 *reup-(撕、抢),同根有英语 rob、reave(bereave)、德语 rauben(抢);分词 ruptus 是这一族的主干。罗马人 rumpere 说撕破衣服、冲破敌阵;rupes 是崖壁——\"破开的地方\"。ab-ruptus 陡断的(abrupt),cor-rumpere 彻底打破——败坏(corrupt),e-ruptio 冲出来(eruption 火山、皮疹),inter-rumpere 从中打断(interrupt),ir-rumpere 闯入(irruption),dis-ruptio 打散(disrupt),ruptura 破裂,bank-rupt 是意大利语 banca rotta——破掉的板凳:中世纪意大利的钱商在长凳上做生意,资不抵债时人们把他的凳子砸了。route 是 via rupta(开辟出来的路),routine 是走惯了的路,rout 是被冲散的队伍。",
    "rumpere (to break) comes from PIE *reup- (to tear, snatch), the root of English rob and reave (bereave), German rauben; the participle ruptus carries the family. Romans rumpere garments and enemy lines; rupes is a cliff — the broken place. ab-ruptus is broken off sheer (abrupt), cor-rumpere broken through and through — corrupt, e-ruptio a breaking out (eruption of a volcano or a rash), inter-rumpere breaking in between (interrupt), ir-rumpere bursting in (irruption), dis-ruptio breaking apart (disrupt), ruptura a rupture; bankrupt is Italian banca rotta, a broken bench — medieval Italian money-changers traded from benches, and when one failed his bench was smashed. A route is via rupta, a road broken through, routine the road one always takes, a rout an army broken and scattered."],
  ann: ["annus(年)来自印欧语 *h₂et-no-(走过的一圈),同根的哥特语 aþn(年)。罗马的年最初只有十个月(March 到 December,所以 September 是\"第七\"),努玛王加了 January 和 February;annales 是祭司长每年记下的大事——编年史(annals),anniversarius 是\"年年回来的\"(anniversary),annuus 每年的(annual),per-ennis 穿过整年的(perennial),bi-ennium 两年,mill-ennium 千年,annona 是一年的收成——罗马的粮食供应,sollemnis 是\"每年整套举行的\"——庄严的仪式(solemn)。annuity 年金,superannuated 是\"超过年限的\"——过时。anno Domini(主的年)是 525 年狄奥尼修斯·艾希古斯创的纪年。",
    "annus (year) comes from PIE *h₂et-no- (a circuit gone through), shared with Gothic aþn (year). Rome's year first had ten months (March to December, which is why September is \"seventh\"); King Numa added January and February; the annales were the yearly record kept by the chief priest — annals; anniversarius is what comes round each year (anniversary), annuus yearly (annual), per-ennis through the whole year (perennial), bi-ennium two years, mill-ennium a thousand, annona the year's harvest — Rome's grain supply, sollemnis \"performed whole every year\" — solemn. An annuity is a yearly payment, the superannuated are past their years. Anno Domini, the year of the Lord, was devised by Dionysius Exiguus in 525."],
  fus: ["fundere(倒、熔、撒)来自印欧语 *ǵʰeu-(倒),同根有希腊语 khein(倒)、khymos(汁——chemistry 的一种词源)、英语 gut、德语 gießen(浇)。罗马人 fundere 倒酒祭神、熔化青铜铸像(fusum 铸件)、也说军队被\"倒出\"——溃散。分词 fusus 给了 fusion 熔合(核聚变),con-fundere 倒在一起——混淆(confuse、confound),dif-fundere 倒开——扩散(diffuse),ef-fundere 倒出——倾泻(effusive 感情横溢),in-fundere 倒进——注入(infuse;funnel 是 infundibulum 漏斗),pro-fundere 大把倒——挥霍(profuse),re-fundere 倒回去——退款(refund),trans-fusio 输血,fondue 是法语\"熔化的\"奶酪,foundry 铸造厂,futile 是 futilis——\"漏水的\"容器,徒劳。",
    "fundere (to pour, melt, scatter) comes from PIE *ǵʰeu- (to pour), shared with Greek khein (to pour) and khymos (juice — one etymology of chemistry), English gut, German gießen. Romans fundere poured wine to the gods, poured molten bronze into statues (a fusum is a casting), and said an army was poured out — routed. The participle fusus gave fusion (nuclear), con-fundere pouring together — confuse, confound, dif-fundere pouring apart — diffuse, ef-fundere pouring out — effusive, in-fundere pouring in — infuse (a funnel is an infundibulum), pro-fundere pouring lavishly — profuse, re-fundere pouring back — refund, trans-fusio transfusion, fondue French \"melted\", a foundry a casting-works, futile from futilis — a leaky vessel, pouring away to no purpose."],
  hum: ["humus(土)来自印欧语 *dʰǵʰem-(地),这是一个极古老的词:希腊语 khthōn(地,chthonic)、khamai(在地上,chameleon 是\"地上的狮子\")、梵语 kṣam、英语 bridegroom 里的 groom(古英语 guma,人=土生的)。homo(人)就是\"土里来的\"——与《创世记》里亚当(adamah,土)的命名逻辑一样;humanus 人的(human、humane 分化于 18 世纪),humilis 贴地的——低下、谦卑(humble、humility),humiliare 按到地上(humiliate),ex-humare 从土里挖出(exhume),in-humare 埋入土(inhume),homicidium 杀人,hombre、homme、uomo 都是这个\"土人\",humour 与此无关(umor,体液)。",
    "humus (soil) comes from PIE *dʰǵʰem- (earth), a very old word: Greek khthōn (earth; chthonic), khamai (on the ground; a chameleon is a \"ground lion\"), Sanskrit kṣam, and the groom of bridegroom (Old English guma, man — the earth-born one). homo (a human) is the earthling — the same logic that named Adam from adamah, soil; humanus is human (human and humane split in the 18th century), humilis close to the ground — lowly, humble (humility), humiliare to press to the ground (humiliate), ex-humare to dig out of the earth (exhume), in-humare to bury (inhume), homicidium manslaying; Spanish hombre, French homme, Italian uomo are all the earthling. Humour is unrelated (umor, bodily fluid)."],
  or: ["os(嘴,属格 oris)来自印欧语 *h₁eh₃s-(嘴),同根有梵语 ās、古挪威语 óss(河口)。罗马人 orare 是\"用嘴\"——先是说、请求,后专指祈祷和演说:orator 演说家(西塞罗自称的身份),oratio 演说、祷文(oration;oratory 是祈祷室,也是演讲术),ad-orare 对着说——崇拜(adore),ex-orare 求出结果——恳求(inexorable 是求不动的),per-orare 说到底(peroration 结语),oraculum 是神\"用嘴\"给的答复(oracle),os 的形容词 oralis 口头的(oral),orifice 是\"做出的口\"——孔,ostium 小口——河口、门(Ostia 是台伯河口的港),oscillate 与此无关(oscillum 小面具)。usher 是 ostiarius(看门人)经法语。",
    "os (mouth, genitive oris) comes from PIE *h₁eh₃s- (mouth), shared with Sanskrit ās and Old Norse óss (a river-mouth). Roman orare was to use the mouth — to speak, to plead, and then specifically to pray and to make speeches: an orator is a public speaker (Cicero's own description of himself), an oratio a speech or a prayer (oration; an oratory is a chapel and also the art of speaking), ad-orare to speak toward — to adore, ex-orare to obtain by pleading (inexorable cannot be moved by pleading), per-orare to speak to the end (peroration), an oraculum the god's spoken answer (oracle); oralis is by mouth (oral), an orifice a made mouth — an opening, ostium a little mouth — a river-mouth or door (Ostia, the port at the Tiber's mouth); an usher is an ostiarius, a doorkeeper, through French."],
  sen: ["senex(老人)来自印欧语 *sen-(老),同根有梵语 sana、希腊语 henos(去年的)、哥特语 sineigs、古爱尔兰语 sen。罗马人最重要的机构就用它命名:senatus 是\"老人们的会议\"(senate),senior 是\"较老的\"(senior;先生 sir、señor、signore、seigneur、sire 全是 senior 经各语言磨损),senectus 老年,senilis 老人的(senile),senescere 变老(senescence),Seneca 这个姓氏是\"老人\"。西班牙语 señor 和法语 monseigneur 说的都是\"我的长者\",英国的 sir 是骑士的尊称——一个词从罗马的白发议员走到了今天的\"先生\"。",
    "senex (an old man) comes from PIE *sen- (old), shared with Sanskrit sana, Greek henos (last year's), Gothic sineigs, Old Irish sen. Rome named its most important institution from it: the senatus is the council of elders (senate); senior is the elder (senior — and sir, señor, signore, seigneur and sire are all senior worn down in different languages), senectus old age, senilis of the old (senile), senescere to grow old (senescence); the surname Seneca means \"old man\". Spanish señor and French monseigneur both say \"my elder\"; the English knight's sir is the same word — from Rome's grey-haired councillors to today's \"mister\"."],
  sol2: ["solus(独自)可能来自印欧语 *swe-(自己,与 se、self 同根)加后缀。罗马人 solus 是一个人;solitudo 是荒野、也是独处(solitude),solitarius 独居的(solitary,solitaire 是一个人玩的牌、也是独粒钻石),soli-loquium 是奥古斯丁造的\"独自说话\"(soliloquy,哈姆雷特的独白),de-solare 是\"弄成没人\"——荒废(desolate),sullen 是 solus 经古法语 solain(孤僻),solo 是意大利语\"独奏\",sole 唯一的(sole survivor),isolate 与此无关(insula 岛)。",
    "solus (alone) may come from PIE *swe- (self, cognate with se and self) with a suffix. Roman solus is by oneself; solitudo is wilderness and also being alone (solitude), solitarius living alone (solitary; solitaire is a game for one and a single set stone), soli-loquium Augustine's coinage for talking alone (soliloquy — Hamlet's), de-solare to leave without people — lay waste (desolate), sullen is solus through Old French solain (withdrawn), solo Italian for a piece for one, sole the only one (sole survivor); isolate is unrelated (insula, island)."],
  vest: ["vestis(衣)来自印欧语 *wes-(穿),同根有希腊语 hennymi(穿)、梵语 vaste、英语 wear、哥特语 wasjan。罗马人 vestire 穿衣,in-vestire 是\"给人穿上\"——授职时给新官穿上礼服(investiture 授职礼;invest 投资是 17 世纪从意大利语 investire 来的:把钱\"穿上\"另一种形式),di-vestire 脱下——剥夺(divest),travesty 是 trans-vestire 换装——滑稽模仿,transvestite 穿异性衣服,vestibulum 是罗马房子的门厅(与 Vesta 女神的炉火有关还是与 vestis 有关有争议),vestry 是教堂放法衣的房间,vest 背心,veste 法语外套。",
    "vestis (clothing) comes from PIE *wes- (to clothe), shared with Greek hennymi (to dress), Sanskrit vaste, English wear, Gothic wasjan. Romans vestire to dress; in-vestire is to clothe someone — a new official was robed at his investiture (invest as in money came in the 17th century from Italian investire: to clothe capital in another form), di-vestire to strip off — deprive (divest), a travesty is trans-vestire, a change of costume — a mocking imitation, a transvestite wears the other sex's clothes, a vestibulum was the entrance hall of a Roman house (whether from Vesta's hearth or from vestis is disputed), a vestry the room where vestments hang, a vest a waistcoat, French veste a jacket."],
  clav: ["clavis(钥匙)与 clavus(钉子)、claudere(关)同根:印欧语 *kleh₂u-(钩、闩),同根有希腊语 kleis(钥匙、锁骨 clavicle——像钥匙的骨头)、德语 Schloss(锁、城堡)与 schließen。罗马人 claudere 关门,分词 clausus 给了 clause(条款——一个\"关起来\"的句子)、cloister(claustrum,关起来的回廊)、closet、close、enclose、conclude(全部关上——得出结论)、exclude(关在外面)、include(关在里面)、recluse(关回去的人)、claustrophobia。clavis 给了 clef(乐谱的谱号,法语\"钥匙\")、clavichord(键弦琴)、conclave(con-clave,\"锁上钥匙\"——教皇选举时把枢机们锁在一起,1274 年起制度化)、enclave(锁在别国里的领土)。clove(丁香)是 clavus——像小钉子。",
    "clavis (a key) belongs with clavus (a nail) and claudere (to shut): PIE *kleh₂u- (a hook, a bar), shared with Greek kleis (key; the clavicle is the key-shaped bone) and German Schloss (lock, castle) and schließen. Romans claudere a door; the participle clausus gave clause (a shut-off sentence), cloister (claustrum, an enclosed walk), closet, close, enclose, conclude (to shut up entirely — reach an end), exclude (shut out), include (shut in), recluse (shut away), claustrophobia. clavis gave clef (French \"key\", in music), clavichord, conclave (con-clave, \"with a key\" — the cardinals locked in to elect a pope, a rule since 1274), enclave (territory locked inside another's). A clove is a clavus, a little nail."],
  front: ["frons(前额,属格 frontis)可能来自印欧语 *bʰren-(突出),词源不确定。罗马人的 frons 是额头,也是任何东西的\"正面\":建筑的 frons 是门面,军队的 frons 是前线。frontalis 前额的,con-frontare 是中世纪拉丁语\"额头对额头\"——对质(confront),af-frontare 是\"冲着脸来\"——冒犯(affront),ef-frons 是\"没有前额\"——没有羞耻感(effrontery),frontier 是法语的\"前线\"——边疆(美国的 frontier 是向西推进的\"前沿\"),frontispiece 是 frontispicium(看正面)——书的卷首插图,forehead 与此无关。frown 也无关(法语 froigner)。",
    "frons (forehead, genitive frontis) may come from PIE *bʰren- (to project), though the origin is uncertain. Roman frons was the brow and also the front of anything: a building's frons is its façade, an army's its front line. frontalis is of the forehead, con-frontare medieval Latin for forehead-to-forehead — to confront, af-frontare to come at the face — to affront, ef-frons \"without a forehead\" — shameless (effrontery), frontier French for the front line — a border (America's frontier was the line pushed west), frontispiece frontispicium, the view of the front — a book's opening picture; forehead and frown are unrelated."],
  prec2: ["pretium(价格、报酬)可能来自印欧语 *per-(交换、卖),同根的希腊语 pernēmi(卖)、porne(妓女——被卖的人)。罗马市场上的 pretium 是价钱;pretiosus 是值钱的(precious),ap-pretiare 是估价(appraise、appreciate——先指估价,后指赏识,18 世纪才有\"升值\"义),de-pretiare 贬值(depreciate),pretium 经法语 pris 成了 price、prize(赢得的\"价值\")、praise(把人估高——称赞)——三个词本是一个。interpres(中间人、译者)是 inter-pret-,\"在两价之间\"——解释者,interpret 由此而来。",
    "pretium (price, reward) may come from PIE *per- (to exchange, sell), shared with Greek pernēmi (to sell) and pornē (a prostitute — one sold). In the Roman market pretium was the price; pretiosus is costly (precious), ap-pretiare to set a price (appraise, appreciate — first to value, then to esteem, and only in the 18th century to rise in value), de-pretiare to lower the price (depreciate); pretium through French pris became price, prize (the value won) and praise (rating someone high) — three words that were once one. An interpres, a go-between or translator, is inter-pret-, one between two prices — the explainer, whence interpret."],
  propr: ["proprius(自己的、特有的)可能来自 pro privo(为个人的)缩合。罗马法区分 proprietas(所有权)与 possessio(占有):你可以占有而不拥有;proprietas 给了 property(财产)和 propriety(得体——\"属于自己的样子\"),proprietor 所有者,ap-propriare 据为己有(appropriate;形容词义\"合适的\"是同一个词——\"归到该归的地方\"),ex-propriare 剥夺所有权,proper 是 proprius 经法语——先指\"自己的\"(proper name 专有名词),后指\"恰当的\",property 在哲学里是\"特性\"。",
    "proprius (one's own, peculiar to) may be a contraction of pro privo, for the individual. Roman law separated proprietas (ownership) from possessio (possession): you can possess without owning; proprietas gave property and propriety (behaving as befits one's own station), a proprietor is an owner, ap-propriare to make one's own (appropriate; the adjective is the same word — assigned where it belongs), ex-propriare to take ownership away, proper is proprius through French — first one's own (a proper name), then fitting, and in philosophy a property is a thing's own quality."],
  quies: ["quies(休息、安静)来自印欧语 *kʷieh₁-(休息),同根有英语 while(一段安歇的时间)、哥特语 hweila、古教会斯拉夫语 pokoj(安宁)。罗马人 quiescere 是歇下来(quiescent),quietus 安静的(quiet;quietus 在英国还指\"清账\"——哈姆雷特说的 his quietus make),tranquillus 是 trans-quillus(彻底安静,tranquil),ac-quiescere 安于——默许(acquiesce),re-quies 安息——Requiem 是安魂弥撒的第一个词 Requiem aeternam(永恒的安息),quit 是 quietus 经法语\"了结、离开\",quite 是\"完全了结\"——完全,coy 是 quietus 的另一法语形状——安静的,后指害羞。",
    "quies (rest, quiet) comes from PIE *kʷieh₁- (to rest), shared with English while (a span of rest), Gothic hweila, Old Church Slavonic pokoj (peace). Romans quiescere to come to rest (quiescent), quietus at rest (quiet; a quietus in England also meant a discharge of debt — Hamlet's \"his quietus make\"), tranquillus is trans-quillus, thoroughly calm (tranquil), ac-quiescere to rest in — to acquiesce, re-quies repose — the Requiem Mass opens with Requiem aeternam, eternal rest, quit is quietus through French, settled, departed, quite is \"wholly settled\" — entirely, coy another French form of quietus — quiet, then bashful."],
  vel: ["拉丁语有两个 vel-。velum(帆、幕)来自 *weg-slo-(织物,与 vexillum 军旗同源):罗马人 velare 是盖上幕布,re-velare 是揭开幕布——revelation 就是\"掀开\",《启示录》是 Apocalypsis 的拉丁译名 Revelatio;veil 面纱,unveil 揭幕,vellum 与此无关(vitulus 小牛皮),voile 法语薄纱。velox(快)来自 *weǵʰ-(运动,与 vehere 同根):velocitas 速度(velocity),velocipede 是 1819 年的\"快脚\"——自行车前身,vélo 是法国人对自行车的叫法。所以 reveal 和 velocity 只是同形。",
    "Latin has two vel-. velum (a sail, a curtain) comes from *weg-slo- (a woven thing, kin to vexillum, the standard): Romans velare covered with a cloth, re-velare drew the curtain back — a revelation is an unveiling, and Revelation is the Latin Revelatio for Apocalypsis; veil, unveil, French voile (vellum is unrelated: vitulus, calfskin). velox (swift) comes from *weǵʰ- (motion, kin to vehere): velocitas is speed (velocity), the velocipede of 1819 was the \"swift-foot\", forerunner of the bicycle, which the French still call a vélo. Reveal and velocity merely look alike."],
  phen: ["希腊语 phainein(显现、照亮)来自印欧语 *bʰeh₂-(发光),同根有 phōs(光,photo-)、拉丁语 fari 无关。phainomenon 是\"显现出来的东西\"——现象(康德使之成为哲学术语),phantasia 是心中显现的影像——想象(fantasy、fancy 是它的缩写),phantasma 幽灵(phantom、fantasm),phasis 显现的样子——月相(phase),em-phasis 是\"让它显出来\"——强调,dia-phanēs 透光的(diaphanous),epi-phaneia 显现——主显节(Epiphany,东方三博士见到圣婴),sycophant 是\"露出无花果的人\"(古雅典的告密者,词源故事有争议),phenotype 显现的型,cellophane 透明纸。",
    "Greek phainein (to show, bring to light) comes from PIE *bʰeh₂- (to shine), shared with phōs (light; photo-). A phainomenon is what appears — a phenomenon (Kant made it a philosophical term), phantasia the image that appears in the mind — fantasy (fancy is its clipping), phantasma a ghost (phantom), phasis the appearance — a phase of the moon, em-phasis \"making it show\" — stress, dia-phanēs letting light through (diaphanous), epi-phaneia a showing-forth — Epiphany, when the Magi saw the child; a sycophant \"shows the fig\" (an Athenian informer — the story behind it is disputed), a phenotype the type that shows, cellophane see-through film."],
  jud: ["iudex(法官)是 ius(法)+ dicere(说)的合成——\"说出法律的人\"。罗马共和国没有职业法官:裁判官(praetor)受理案件,再指定一位公民 iudex 去审理事实。iudicium 是审判(judgement),iudicare 判断(judge),prae-iudicium 是\"事先的判断\"——先例,后来指偏见(prejudice),ad-iudicare 判给(adjudicate),iudiciarius 司法的(judiciary),Judas 与此无关(希伯来语犹大)。judicious 明智,judgemental 好评判的。ius 本身来自 *h₂yew-(约束、誓言)——法律先是一句有约束力的话。",
    "iudex (a judge) is ius (law) + dicere (to say) — one who speaks the law. The Roman Republic had no professional judges: the praetor accepted a case and appointed a citizen iudex to try the facts. iudicium is a trial, a judgement, iudicare to judge, prae-iudicium a judgement made beforehand — a precedent, later a prejudice, ad-iudicare to award (adjudicate), iudiciarius of the courts (judiciary); Judas is unrelated (Hebrew Judah). Judicious is wise in judging, judgemental quick to judge. ius itself comes from *h₂yew- (to bind, an oath) — law was first a binding word."],
  aug: ["augere(增加)来自印欧语 *h₂eug-(增长),同根有英语 eke(增补,nickname 是 an eke-name 的误分)、wax(月盈)、德语 wachsen、希腊语 auxanein、梵语 ojas(力)。auctor 是\"使增长的人\"——创始人、担保人、作者(author),auctoritas 是创始人的分量——权威(authority),augur 是占鸟卜的祭司(\"使增长\"还是\"鸟\"avis 有争议),inaugurare 是占卜后正式开启(inaugurate),Augustus 是\"被神增福的\"——屋大维前 27 年获此尊号,August 八月随之改名,auxilium 是增援——帮助(auxiliary 辅助的),auctio 是\"加价\"——拍卖(auction),augment 增加。",
    "augere (to increase) comes from PIE *h₂eug- (to grow), shared with English eke (to add; a nickname is an eke-name misdivided), wax (of the moon), German wachsen, Greek auxanein, Sanskrit ojas (strength). An auctor is one who makes grow — a founder, a guarantor, an author; auctoritas the founder's weight — authority; an augur the bird-omen priest (whether from \"increase\" or from avis, bird, is disputed), inaugurare to open formally after taking the auspices (inaugurate); Augustus, \"increased by the gods\", was the title Octavian took in 27 BC, and the month of August was renamed for him; auxilium is reinforcement — help (auxiliary), auctio a bidding-up — an auction, augment an increase."],
  hab: ["habere(有、握)来自印欧语 *gʰh₁bʰ-(抓、给),与英语 give、德语 geben 同根——同一个根在一边是\"给\",另一边是\"有\"(因为握在手里)。habitus 是\"有的样子\"——体态、习惯(habit;修士的 habit 是他\"惯常穿的\"),habitare 是反复\"有\"——居住(inhabit、habitat 是\"它住在\"),habilis 是\"好拿的\"——能干(able 经法语磨损,ability),de-bere 是 de-habere\"从别人那里有\"——欠(debt、debit、duty 经法语 deu),ex-hibere 拿出来(exhibit),pro-hibere 拿开——禁止(prohibit),in-hibere 拿住——抑制(inhibit),prae-bere 拿到前面——提供(prebend),avoir、haber、avere 都是 habere。",
    "habere (to have, hold) comes from PIE *gʰh₁bʰ- (to grasp, give), cognate with English give and German geben — one root meaning give on one side and have on the other, since what is given is held. habitus is the way one has oneself — bearing, habit (a monk's habit is what he habitually wears), habitare to have repeatedly — to dwell (inhabit; a habitat is \"it dwells\"), habilis handy — capable (able, worn down through French; ability), de-bere is de-habere, to have from another — to owe (debt, debit, duty via French deu), ex-hibere to hold out (exhibit), pro-hibere to hold off (prohibit), in-hibere to hold in (inhibit), prae-bere to hold forth (prebend); French avoir, Spanish haber, Italian avere are all habere."],
  ess: ["esse(是)是印欧语 *h₁es-,欧洲最基本的动词:梵语 asti、希腊语 esti、英语 is、德语 ist。它在拉丁语里没有分词,中世纪哲学家从希腊语 ousia 仿造了 essentia(本质,essence)和 ens(存在者,entity);现在分词 -ens/-ent 出现在 prae-sens 在前面的——现在、在场(present),ab-sens 离开的(absent),inter-esse 是\"在其间\"——有关系、有利益(interest 先是\"利害\",后指利息与兴趣),pro-esse 有益(prodesse),super-esse 剩下(superessent),quint-essentia 是亚里士多德的\"第五元素\"以太——精髓(quintessence)。est 是法语、意大利语的\"是\",es 是西班牙语的。",
    "esse (to be) is Indo-European *h₁es-, Europe's most basic verb: Sanskrit asti, Greek esti, English is, German ist. Latin gave it no participle, so medieval philosophers coined essentia (essence) after Greek ousia and ens (a being, entity); the present participle -ens/-ent shows in prae-sens, being before — present, ab-sens being away — absent, inter-esse being between — to concern, to profit (interest was first a stake, then money on a loan, then curiosity), pro-esse to be of use, super-esse to be left over, quint-essentia Aristotle's fifth element, the ether — the purest part (quintessence). est is French and Italian \"is\", es Spanish."],
  jur: ["ius(法、权利,属格 iuris)来自印欧语 *h₂yew-(约束、誓言),同根有梵语 yos(健康、幸福)、阿维斯陀语 yaoš。罗马人的 ius 先是一句有约束力的话:iurare 是立誓(iurare in verba magistri,照老师的话发誓),iustus 合法的(just、justice),iuris-prudentia 法律的智慧(jurisprudence),iuris-dictio 说法的权力(jurisdiction),iurator 宣誓的人——jury 是宣誓的陪审团(法语 jurée),con-iurare 一起发誓——密谋(conjure 后来指念咒,因为召唤魔鬼要\"以誓约束\"),ab-iurare 发誓放弃(abjure),per-iurium 违誓(perjury),in-iuria 不法——伤害(injury 先指不公,后指伤害),justify 使合法。",
    "ius (law, right, genitive iuris) comes from PIE *h₂yew- (to bind, an oath), shared with Sanskrit yos (well-being) and Avestan yaoš. Roman ius was first a binding formula: iurare is to swear (iurare in verba magistri, to swear by the master's words), iustus lawful (just, justice), iuris-prudentia the wisdom of law (jurisprudence), iuris-dictio the power to speak the law (jurisdiction), a iurator one who swears — a jury is the sworn panel (French jurée), con-iurare to swear together — to plot (conjure later meant to cast spells, since a demon is bound by an oath), ab-iurare to swear off (abjure), per-iurium a false oath (perjury), in-iuria a wrong — an injury (first injustice, then hurt), justify to make lawful."],
  mir: ["mirus(奇异的)与 mirari(惊奇)来自印欧语 *smei-(笑、微笑),同根有英语 smile、smirk、梵语 smayate(微笑)——惊奇最初是脸上的笑。罗马人 mirari 是惊叹,ad-mirari 是朝着惊叹——钦佩(admire),miraculum 是令人惊奇的事——奇迹(miracle),mirabilis 奇妙的(marvel 是它经法语 merveille),mirator 惊叹者,speculum 与此无关但 miroir(镜子)来自 mirare——盯着看(mirror、mirage 是沙漠里让人看呆的幻影),Miranda 是\"该被赞叹的女子\"(莎士比亚为《暴风雨》取的名),Mirabeau 美景。",
    "mirus (wonderful) and mirari (to wonder) come from PIE *smei- (to laugh, smile), shared with English smile and smirk and Sanskrit smayate (smiles) — wonder was first a smile on the face. Romans mirari to marvel, ad-mirari to wonder toward — admire, miraculum a thing to wonder at — a miracle, mirabilis wonderful (marvel is its French form merveille); French miroir comes from mirare, to gaze at (mirror, and a mirage is the desert vision one gazes at); Miranda is \"she who should be admired\" (Shakespeare's coinage for The Tempest), Mirabeau a fine view."],
  hor: ["horrere(毛发竖起、颤抖)来自印欧语 *ǵʰers-(竖起、僵硬),同根有梵语 hṛṣyati(毛发竖立、兴奋)、拉丁语 hirsutus(多毛的)、ericius(刺猬)。罗马人的 horror 首先是身体反应——寒战、毛骨悚然;horridus 是\"毛发竖立的\"——粗糙可怕(horrid),horribilis 可怕的(horrible),ab-horrere 从…缩回(abhor),hor-rendus 令人战栗的(horrendous)。ordeal 与此无关,但 hedgehog 的拉丁语 ericius 就是\"竖毛的\"。恐怖 horror 一词在英语里到 18 世纪才用于文学类型。",
    "horrere (to bristle, shudder) comes from PIE *ǵʰers- (to stand on end, stiffen), shared with Sanskrit hṛṣyati (hair stands up, thrills), Latin hirsutus (hairy) and ericius (hedgehog). Roman horror was first a bodily thing — a shiver, hair standing on end; horridus is bristling — rough, dreadful (horrid), horribilis dreadful (horrible), ab-horrere to shrink back from (abhor), hor-rendus making one shudder (horrendous). The hedgehog's Latin name, ericius, is the bristler. Horror named a literary genre in English only in the 18th century."],
  bas: ["希腊语 basis 来自 bainein(走、站)——\"脚踩的地方\"、底座;它与拉丁语 venire 同根(印欧语 *gʷem-)。罗马人借来 basis 指柱础、也指几何的底边(base);basilica 与此无关——那是 basileus(国王)的\"王家厅\",罗马的公共大厅,后成教堂样式。低劣的 base 是另一个词:晚期拉丁语 bassus(矮、低)——bass 低音、basset 短腿犬、abase 贬低、debase 贬值、bassoon 低音管(意大利语 bassone)。所以 basis 和 bass 只是撞在一起。",
    "Greek basis comes from bainein (to step, stand) — the place the foot stands, a pedestal; it shares PIE *gʷem- with Latin venire. Romans borrowed basis for the foot of a column and the base line of a figure; basilica is unrelated — the king's (basileus) hall, Rome's public hall, later a church type. Base meaning low is yet another word: late Latin bassus (short, low) — bass the low voice, the short-legged basset, abase, debase, bassoon (Italian bassone). Basis and bass merely collided."],
  cruc: ["crux(十字架,属格 crucis)是拉丁语独有的词,可能借自腓尼基语——罗马人把钉十字架当作奴隶和叛乱者的酷刑(前 71 年斯巴达克斯的六千追随者被钉在阿庇亚大道两旁)。crucifigere 钉上十字架(crucify),cruciatus 折磨(excruciating 是\"从十字架上来的\"痛),crucialis 是十字形的——18 世纪培根、牛顿说 experimentum crucis(十字路口的实验,决定性的)给了 crucial;crusade 是十字军(西班牙语 cruzada),cruise 是荷兰语 kruisen——\"划十字\"地来回航行,cross 经古挪威语进入英语,crux(问题的症结)是\"十字路口\",cruciform 十字形。",
    "crux (a cross, genitive crucis) is a purely Latin word, perhaps from Phoenician — the Romans used crucifixion as the punishment for slaves and rebels (six thousand of Spartacus' followers were crucified along the Appian Way in 71 BC). crucifigere is to fix on a cross (crucify), cruciatus torture (excruciating pain \"comes from the cross\"), crucialis cross-shaped — Bacon's and Newton's experimentum crucis, the decisive experiment at the crossroads, gave crucial; a crusade is Spanish cruzada, cruise is Dutch kruisen — sailing back and forth in a cross, cross reached English through Old Norse, the crux of a matter is its crossroads, cruciform cross-shaped."],
  dec: ["decet(合适、得体)与 decus(荣誉)、decor(得体、美观)来自印欧语 *deḱ-(接受、合适),同根有 dignus(配得上的)、docere(教)、希腊语 dokein(看起来合适——dogma、doxa)。罗马人的 decorum 是西塞罗译希腊语 prepon 的\"合宜\"——言行与身份相称;decorare 是使体面——装饰(decorate),decor 装潢,decent 是\"合适的\"(decent 到 16 世纪才指端庄),digni-tas 尊严,dignari 认为配得上(deign 经法语),in-dignari 觉得不配——愤慨(indignant),con-dign 相称的,decorous 得体。",
    "decet (it is fitting) with decus (honour) and decor (seemliness) come from PIE *deḱ- (to take, to be fitting), shared with dignus (worthy), docere (to teach) and Greek dokein (to seem fitting — dogma, doxa). Roman decorum was Cicero's word for Greek prepon — what befits one's role; decorare is to make seemly — decorate, decor is the fitting-out of a room, decent meant fitting (only in the 16th century respectable), digni-tas worth (dignity), dignari to think worthy (deign, through French), in-dignari to think unworthy — indignant, condign well-matched, decorous seemly."],
  dol: ["dolere(痛、悲)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能与 dolare(砍削)同源:痛是被砍的感觉。dolor 是痛,也是悲伤(dolour;Dolores 是圣母七苦的名字 María de los Dolores),con-dolere 一起痛(condolence 吊唁),in-dolentia 是\"不痛\"——伊壁鸠鲁派的无痛状态,17 世纪转成懒散(indolent),doleful 悲伤的,意大利语 duolo、法语 deuil(丧)、西班牙语 duelo(哀悼)都是 dolus,dolorous 痛苦的。Via Dolorosa 是耶路撒冷的\"苦路\"。",
    "dolere (to hurt, grieve) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin — perhaps akin to dolare (to hew): pain as the feeling of being cut. dolor is pain and also grief (dolour; Dolores is from María de los Dolores, Our Lady of Sorrows), con-dolere to grieve with (condolence), in-dolentia \"freedom from pain\" — the Epicurean ideal, turned in the 17th century into laziness (indolent), doleful sorrowful; Italian duolo, French deuil (mourning), Spanish duelo (grief) are all dolus, dolorous painful. The Via Dolorosa is Jerusalem's way of sorrows."],
  fil: ["filum(线)可能来自印欧语 *gʷʰih₁-(筋、线),同根有立陶宛语 gijà(线)、亚美尼亚语 jil。罗马织工的 filum 是纱线;filare 纺线(晚期拉丁语),filamentum 细丝(filament 灯丝),filigranum 是意大利语\"线+颗粒\"的金银细工(filigree),file(文件)是法语 fil——把文书穿在一根线上归档,defile(纵队)是一线穿行,profile 是意大利语 profilare——沿着轮廓\"画线\",fillet 是法语 filet 小带子、肉条,filet mignon 精致小条,enfilade 是一线扫射。filius(儿子)是另一个词。",
    "filum (a thread) may come from PIE *gʷʰih₁- (sinew, thread), shared with Lithuanian gijà (thread) and Armenian jil. A Roman weaver's filum was yarn; filare (late Latin) to spin, filamentum a fine thread (a lamp filament), filigranum Italian \"thread and grain\" — filigree, a file (of papers) is French fil — documents threaded on a string, a defile is marching in single file, a profile Italian profilare — drawing the outline in a line, a fillet French filet, a little band or strip of meat, filet mignon a dainty strip, enfilade fire along a line. filius, son, is a different word."],
  plat: ["希腊语 platys(宽、平)来自印欧语 *pleth₂-(展开),同根有拉丁语 planus、英语 flat(经古挪威语)、field、梵语 pṛthu(宽——地母 Pṛthivī 是\"宽广者\")。柏拉图 Platōn 的名字就是\"宽的\"(据说因肩膀宽或额头宽)。platea 是宽街——广场(place、plaza、piazza),plat 经法语给了 plate(平板)、plateau(高原)、platform(法语 plate-forme,平的形状)、platter(大平盘)、platinum(西班牙语 platina,\"小银\"——plata 是银,因为银是打成平片的),plane tree 悬铃木是 platanos(宽叶),platitude 是\"平淡\"——陈词滥调,plait 与此无关(plicare)。",
    "Greek platys (broad, flat) comes from PIE *pleth₂- (to spread), shared with Latin planus, English flat (via Old Norse) and field, Sanskrit pṛthu (broad — Earth is Pṛthivī, the broad one). Plato's name Platōn means \"broad\" (from his shoulders or his brow, tradition says). platea is a broad street — a square (place, plaza, piazza), and through French plat came plate, plateau, platform (plate-forme, flat shape), platter, platinum (Spanish platina, little silver — plata is silver, beaten flat), the plane tree platanos (broad-leaved), platitude flatness — a stale remark; plait is unrelated (plicare)."],
  riv: ["ripa(河岸)来自印欧语 *rei-(切、撕),同根有英语 rive、rift、德语 Reihe(排)——河岸是水切出的一道边。罗马人说 ripa 是河岸(river 是 riparia\"岸边地\"经法语 rivière,所以英语 river 本义是岸而非水),rivus 是溪流(rival 是 rivalis——\"共用一条溪的人\",邻居争水就成了对手;de-rivare 是从河里引水出来——派生 derive,ar-rivare 是\"到岸\"——arrive),riparian 岸边的,Riviera 是意大利语\"海岸\",riverain 沿河居民。所以 rival 和 river、arrive 都是水边的事。",
    "ripa (a river-bank) comes from PIE *rei- (to cut, tear), shared with English rive and rift and German Reihe (a row) — the bank is the edge the water cuts. Roman ripa is the bank (river is riparia, bank-land, through French rivière, so English river originally meant the shore, not the water); rivus is a stream (a rivalis is one who shares a stream — neighbours quarrelling over water became rivals; de-rivare is drawing water off from the stream — derive; ar-rivare is coming to the bank — arrive), riparian along the bank, Riviera Italian for coast. Rival, river and arrive are all business at the water's edge."],
  vic: ["拉丁语有两个 vic-。vicis(轮换、代替,无主格)来自印欧语 *weik-(弯、换),同根有英语 weak(易弯)、week(轮换的周期)、德语 Wechsel(交换)、wechseln:vicissitudo 轮换——变迁(vicissitude),vice versa 位置对调,vice- 是\"代替\"(viceroy 代王,vice-president),vicarius 代理人——vicar 是代替主教的教区牧师、教皇是 Vicarius Christi,vicarious 是\"替代体验\"。vicus(村、街区)来自 *weik-(住处),同根有希腊语 oikos(家,eco-)、英语 -wick(Warwick)、梵语 veśa:vicinus 邻居(vicinity),villa 是 vicus 的小型——农庄(village、villain 本是村民)。恶习 vice 是 vitium,又一个词。",
    "Latin has two vic-. vicis (a turn, a change, no nominative) comes from PIE *weik- (to bend, exchange), shared with English weak (bendable) and week (a turning cycle), German Wechsel (exchange): vicissitudo is alternation — vicissitude, vice versa the positions turned, vice- \"in place of\" (viceroy, vice-president), vicarius a substitute — a vicar stands in for the bishop and the pope is Vicarius Christi, vicarious means experienced in another's place. vicus (a village, a quarter) comes from *weik- (a dwelling), shared with Greek oikos (house; eco-), English -wick (Warwick), Sanskrit veśa: vicinus a neighbour (vicinity), villa a small vicus — a farmstead (village; a villain was originally a villager). Vice the fault is vitium, a third word."],
  cardi: ["希腊语 kardia(心)来自印欧语 *ḱerd-,与拉丁语 cor、英语 heart、德语 Herz 是同一个词的三种发音。希腊医生把它当作情感与热的中心,亚里士多德认为心是思维的座位(大脑只是冷却器)。kardia-kos 心脏的(cardiac),tachy-kardia 快心,brady-kardia 慢心,myo-kardium 心肌,peri-kardion 心包——这些都是 19 世纪医学用希腊词素新造的;cardio-logy 1847 年出现。cardinal(枢机、基本的)与此无关——那是拉丁语 cardo(门轴)。cardiogram 是 1876 年的\"心的书写\"。",
    "Greek kardia (heart) comes from PIE *ḱerd-, one word with Latin cor, English heart and German Herz. Greek physicians made it the seat of emotion and heat; Aristotle placed thought there too (the brain being merely a cooler). kardia-kos is of the heart (cardiac), tachy-kardia a fast heart, brady-kardia a slow one, myo-kardium the heart muscle, peri-kardion the sac around it — all 19th-century medical coinages from Greek parts; cardio-logy appeared in 1847. Cardinal is unrelated — Latin cardo, a door-hinge. A cardiogram is the 1876 \"writing of the heart\"."],
  phot: ["希腊语 phōs(光,属格 phōtos)来自印欧语 *bʰeh₂-(发光),与 phainein(显现)同根。它几乎全是现代造词:photo-graphia 是 1839 年约翰·赫歇尔为达盖尔和塔尔博特的新技术起的名——\"用光书写\";photon 是 1926 年物理学家给光量子的名,photo-synthesis 1898 年,photo-copy 1924 年,photo-genic 先指\"发光的\",1928 年才指\"上镜\"。phosphorus(磷)是\"带光者\"——1669 年布兰德从尿里提炼出的发光物,也是希腊语对晨星的称呼(拉丁语 Lucifer 的对译)。",
    "Greek phōs (light, genitive phōtos) comes from PIE *bʰeh₂- (to shine), kin to phainein (to appear). Its family is almost entirely modern coinage: photo-graphia was John Herschel's 1839 name for the new art of Daguerre and Talbot — writing with light; the photon was named in 1926 for the quantum of light, photo-synthesis in 1898, photo-copy in 1924, photo-genic meant luminous before it meant camera-friendly in 1928. Phosphorus is the light-bearer — the glowing substance Brand extracted from urine in 1669, and also the Greek name of the morning star (Latin Lucifer)."],
  gram: ["希腊语 gramma(字母、写下的东西)来自 graphein(刻、写)——与 carve 同根。grammatikē 是\"字母的技艺\"——语法,罗马人借为 grammatica,中世纪它是三艺之首、一切学问的入口,所以 grammar 在苏格兰变成 glamour(魔法——识字者被当作会法术);pro-gramma 是公开写出的告示(program),dia-gramma 划出的图,ana-gramma 字母倒过来,epi-gramma 刻在物上的短句——警句,tele-gramma 远处传来的字(1852 年),kilo-gram 1795 年法国的\"千克\"(gramma 在晚期希腊语指小重量单位),mono-gram 一个字母的花押,grimoire(魔法书)是法语 grammaire 的变体。",
    "Greek gramma (a letter, a thing written) comes from graphein (to scratch, write) — kin to carve. grammatikē was the art of letters — grammar, borrowed by Rome as grammatica; in the Middle Ages it headed the trivium, the gate to all learning, which is why in Scotland grammar became glamour (magic — the lettered were suspected of spells); a pro-gramma is a notice written up in public (program), a dia-gramma a drawn figure, an ana-gramma letters turned about, an epi-gramma a line carved on an object — an epigram, a tele-gramma letters from afar (1852), kilo-gram France's 1795 thousand-gram (gramma named a small weight in late Greek), a mono-gram one letter's flourish, and a grimoire is a variant of French grammaire — a book of spells."],
  civ: ["civis(公民)来自印欧语 *ḱei-(躺、安居、家),同根有英语 home、hamlet、德语 Heim、希腊语 koitē(床)、梵语 śiva(亲切的)——公民本是\"同一家/同一屋檐下的人\"。罗马公民权是可以授予的身份:保罗因生为罗马公民而免受鞭刑;212 年卡拉卡拉把公民权给了帝国全体自由人。civitas 是公民全体——城邦(city 经法语 cité),civilis 公民的、有礼的(civil、civility;civil war 是公民之间的战争),civilizatio 是 18 世纪法国人造的\"文明\"——公民社会的状态,civicus 公民的(civic),civilian 平民是与军人相对的\"民法之人\"。",
    "civis (a citizen) comes from PIE *ḱei- (to lie, settle, home), shared with English home and hamlet, German Heim, Greek koitē (bed), Sanskrit śiva (kindly) — a citizen was one of the same household. Roman citizenship was a grantable status: Paul, born a citizen, could not be flogged; in 212 Caracalla gave it to every free inhabitant of the Empire. civitas is the body of citizens — a city (city through French cité), civilis of citizens, courteous (civil, civility; a civil war is one between fellow citizens), civilizatio an 18th-century French coinage for the state of a civil society, civicus civic, and a civilian is a person of the civil law, as against the soldier."],
  doc: ["docere(教)来自印欧语 *deḱ-(接受、合适),同根有 decet(合适)、dignus(配得上)、希腊语 dokein(看来、以为,dogma 是\"看来对的\"、doxa 意见)——教就是让人接受。doctor 本是\"教的人\":中世纪大学的 doctor 是有资格教书的学位,医生义是 14 世纪后从 doctor of physic 缩来的;doctrina 是教的内容(doctrine),documentum 是教训、证明(document 从\"教材\"变成\"文件\"),docilis 可教的(docile),dis-cere 是 di-dc-ere 学(disciple、discipline 是学生受的训练),dogma 教义,orthodox 正见,paradox 违背常见。",
    "docere (to teach) comes from PIE *deḱ- (to take, to be fitting), shared with decet (it suits), dignus (worthy), Greek dokein (to seem, to think — a dogma is what seems right, doxa an opinion) — to teach is to make accepted. A doctor was first a teacher: the medieval doctorate licensed one to teach, and the physician sense was clipped in the 14th century from doctor of physic; doctrina is what is taught (doctrine), documentum a lesson, a proof (document went from teaching-text to paper), docilis teachable (docile), dis-cere is di-dc-ere, to learn (a disciple; discipline is the learner's training), dogma a teaching, orthodox right opinion, paradox against opinion."],
  pet: ["petere(奔向、寻求、请求)来自印欧语 *pet-(飞、扑),同根有希腊语 petesthai(飞)、pteron(翅膀——helicopter 是\"螺旋翅\")、ptōma(掉落的,symptom 是\"一起掉下来的\"——征候)、英语 feather。罗马候选人 petere 官职——奔走求官;petitio 请求(petition),com-petere 一起奔向同一目标——竞争(compete),也指\"符合\"(competent 是\"够得着的\"),ap-petitus 扑向食物的欲望(appetite),im-petus 扑击的冲力(impetus、impetuous),re-petere 再去要(repeat),per-petuus 一直向前(perpetual),centri-petal 向心的,propitious 是\"扑向前来的\"——顺利。",
    "petere (to rush at, seek, ask) comes from PIE *pet- (to fly, fall upon), shared with Greek petesthai (to fly), pteron (a wing — a helicopter is a spiral-wing), ptōma (a fall; a symptom is what falls together with a disease) and English feather. A Roman candidate petere office — ran for it; a petitio is a request (petition), com-petere rushing together at one goal — compete, and also to coincide (competent means reaching the mark), ap-petitus the rush at food (appetite), im-petus the force of the charge (impetus, impetuous), re-petere to seek again (repeat), per-petuus going on and on (perpetual), centri-petal seeking the centre, propitious rushing forward favourably."],
  mun: ["munus(职责、礼物、公共演出)来自印欧语 *mei-(交换、约束),同根有 communis、英语 mean(共同的,古义)、德语 gemein、哥特语 gamains——礼物与义务是交换的两面。罗马的 munus 既是公民对城市的义务,也是有钱人回馈城市的礼物——角斗士表演就叫 munus(最初是葬礼上的献祭);muni-cipium 是\"承担义务的城\"——自治市(municipal),com-munis 共同承担的(common、community、communicate 是使成为共同的,communion 共享),im-munis 免除义务的(immune 从免税到免疫是 19 世纪的转义),re-munerare 回赠——报酬(remunerate),munificent 大方送礼的,munitio 是筑墙的\"工事\"——ammunition(法语 la munition 误分为 l'amunition)。",
    "munus (a duty, a gift, a public show) comes from PIE *mei- (to exchange, bind), shared with communis, English mean (common, in its old sense), German gemein, Gothic gamains — gift and obligation are two faces of exchange. A Roman munus was the citizen's duty to the city and the rich man's gift back to it — gladiatorial games were a munus (originally a funeral offering); a muni-cipium is a town that takes on duties (municipal), com-munis shared in duty (common, community; communicate is to make common, communion sharing), im-munis exempt from duty (immune shifted from tax to disease in the 19th century), re-munerare to gift back — remunerate, munificent lavish in giving, munitio is fortification-work — ammunition (French la munition misdivided as l'amunition)."],
  cav: ["cavus(空的、凹的)来自印欧语 *ḱouH-(空、胀),同根有希腊语 koilos(空——腹腔 coeliac)、kyar(洞)、英语 hole 的关系有争议。罗马人的 cavea 是圈动物的笼子、也是剧场的观众席(挖进山坡的凹面),cava 地窖,caverna 洞穴(cavern),cavitas 空腔(cavity),ex-cavare 挖空(excavate),con-cavus 凹的(concave),cave 是法语的洞,cage 是 cavea 经法语,jail/gaol 是 caveola(小笼)经诺曼法语和巴黎法语两条路——所以英语有两种拼法,decoy 与此无关。cavalry 也无关(caballus 马)。",
    "cavus (hollow) comes from PIE *ḱouH- (hollow, swelling), shared with Greek koilos (hollow — coeliac, of the belly) and kyar (a hole); a link with English hole is disputed. A Roman cavea was an animal's cage and also the theatre's seating (a hollow dug into a hillside), a cava a cellar, caverna a cavern, cavitas a cavity, ex-cavare to hollow out (excavate), con-cavus concave; cave is French, cage is cavea through French, and jail and gaol are caveola, a little cage, arriving by two French routes — Norman and Parisian — which is why English spells it two ways. Cavalry is unrelated (caballus, horse)."],
  dei: ["deus(神)与 divus(神圣的)、dies(天、日)同根:印欧语 *dyeu-(明亮的天空)——天父 *Dyēus ph₂tēr 是印欧人的主神:梵语 Dyaus pitar、希腊语 Zeus patēr、拉丁语 Iu-piter、日耳曼语 Tīwaz(Tuesday 的 Tiw)。所以 deus 本义是\"天上发光的\";dea 女神,deitas 神性(deity,教会拉丁语),divinus 神的(divine;占卜 divination 是问神),divus 是被神化的皇帝(Divus Iulius),deus ex machina 是希腊戏剧里吊机降下的神;adieu、adiós 是\"交给神\";Dio、Dieu、Dios 都是它;deism 是 17 世纪的理神论。",
    "deus (god) shares its root with divus (divine) and dies (day, daylight): PIE *dyeu- (the bright sky) — the Sky-Father *Dyēus ph₂tēr was the Indo-Europeans' chief god: Sanskrit Dyaus pitar, Greek Zeus patēr, Latin Iu-piter, Germanic Tīwaz (the Tiw of Tuesday). So deus means the shining one of the sky; dea a goddess, deitas godhead (deity, Church Latin), divinus of the gods (divine; divination asks them), divus a deified emperor (Divus Iulius), the deus ex machina the god lowered by crane in Greek theatre; adieu and adiós commend you to God; Dio, Dieu, Dios are all this word; deism is the 17th-century religion of reason."],
  mol: ["拉丁语有两个 mol-。moles(巨块、堤坝)词源不明:罗马人用 moles 说巨大的建筑工程(哈德良陵墓 Moles Hadriani);molestus 是\"压人的\"——烦扰(molest 先指骚扰,20 世纪才转为性侵),de-moliri 拆掉巨块(demolish),molecula 是 17 世纪的\"小块\"(molecule),mole 防波堤。mola(磨盘)来自印欧语 *melh₂-(磨),同根有英语 meal(面粉)、mill、德语 mahlen、希腊语 mylē:molere 磨,molaris 磨牙(molar),im-molare 是献祭前在牲口头上撒 mola salsa(咸麦粉)——献祭(immolate),emolument 是磨坊主的\"磨粉费\"——报酬。",
    "Latin has two mol-. moles (a mass, a dam) is of unknown origin: Romans used it for huge structures (Hadrian's tomb was the Moles Hadriani); molestus is burdensome — troublesome (molest meant to harass until the 20th century), de-moliri to pull the mass down (demolish), molecula a 17th-century little mass (molecule), a mole a breakwater. mola (a millstone) comes from PIE *melh₂- (to grind), shared with English meal (flour) and mill, German mahlen, Greek mylē: molere to grind, molaris a grinding tooth (molar), im-molare to sprinkle the victim with mola salsa, salted meal, before sacrifice (immolate), emolument the miller's grinding-fee — a salary."],
  prox: ["prope(近)与 proximus(最近的)词源不明,可能与 pro(前)有关。罗马人说 proximus 是邻居、近亲(法语 prochain 邻人),ap-propinquare 接近(approach 经法语 approcher),ap-proximare 弄到最近(approximate 近似),proximitas 邻近(proximity),proximo mense 下个月(商业信件里的 prox.),reproach 是 re-propiare——\"拉近到面前\"责备,rapprochement 法语的重新接近,propinquity 亲近。近 near 与此无关。",
    "prope (near) and proximus (nearest) are of uncertain origin, perhaps linked to pro (forward). Roman proximus was a neighbour or close kin (French prochain, one's fellow), ap-propinquare to draw near (approach, through French approcher), ap-proximare to bring nearest (approximate), proximitas nearness (proximity), proximo mense next month (the prox. of old business letters), reproach is re-propiare — bringing something up close to someone's face, rapprochement French for coming near again, propinquity closeness. Near is unrelated."],
  corp: ["corpus(身体)来自印欧语 *kʷrep-(身体、形),同根有梵语 kṛp(形)、古英语 hrif(腹)、德语 Körper 是借词。罗马法把 corpus 用于\"团体\"——corpus 是法人的雏形;corporatio 是中世纪的行会(corporation),in-corporare 并入身体(incorporate),corporalis 身体的(corporal 肉体的——corporal punishment;下士 corporal 是另一个词,caput),corpulentus 多肉的(corpulent),corpusculum 小体,corpse 是 corpus 经法语 cors(尸体义 14 世纪固定),corps 法语军团,corset 小身衣,corsage 上身,corpus 文本语料是 19 世纪语文学用法,Corpus Christi 基督圣体节。",
    "corpus (body) comes from PIE *kʷrep- (body, form), shared with Sanskrit kṛp (form) and Old English hrif (belly); German Körper is a borrowing. Roman law applied corpus to a group — the germ of the legal person; a corporatio was a medieval guild (corporation), in-corporare to take into the body (incorporate), corporalis bodily (corporal punishment; the rank corporal is another word, from caput), corpulentus fleshy (corpulent), corpusculum a little body, corpse is corpus through French cors (fixed as dead body by the 14th century), corps the French military body, corset a little body-garment, corsage the bodice, a corpus of texts a 19th-century philologists' term, Corpus Christi the feast of Christ's body."],
  dent: ["dens(牙,属格 dentis)来自印欧语 *h₃dont-(牙,本是\"吃\"的分词),同根有希腊语 odous(odont-)、英语 tooth、德语 Zahn、梵语 dant。dentalis 牙的(dental),dentista 牙医是 18 世纪法语,dentatus 有齿的(indent 是把边缘弄成锯齿——缩进;indenture 是一式两份沿锯齿线撕开的契约),tri-dens 三齿——海神的叉(trident),dandelion 是法语 dent de lion(狮子牙,叶缘锯齿),denticle 小齿,dentures 假牙,al dente 意大利语\"有嚼劲\"——牙齿能感觉到,edentate 无齿的。",
    "dens (tooth, genitive dentis) comes from PIE *h₃dont- (tooth — originally the participle of \"eat\"), shared with Greek odous (odont-), English tooth, German Zahn, Sanskrit dant. dentalis is of the teeth (dental), dentiste 18th-century French, dentatus toothed (to indent is to give an edge teeth — hence the indentation; an indenture was a contract written twice and torn apart along a toothed line), tri-dens three-toothed — Neptune's fork, dandelion French dent de lion, lion's tooth, for its jagged leaves, denticle a little tooth, dentures false teeth, al dente Italian \"to the tooth\" — with bite, edentate toothless."],
  cand: ["candere(发白光、灼热)来自印欧语 *kand-(发光),同根有梵语 candra(月亮、发光的)、威尔士语 cann(白)。罗马候选人穿用白垩漂得雪白的托加袍——candidatus 就是\"穿白袍的人\"(candidate);candidus 是纯白的——坦率(candid,candour 坦诚是\"白\"),candela 蜡烛(candle,chandelier 法语烛台),in-candescere 烧到发白(incandescent),candor 白、光辉,cinders 与此无关(古英语)。Candida(念珠菌)是\"白色的\",Candide 是伏尔泰的\"老实人\"。",
    "candere (to glow white, be hot) comes from PIE *kand- (to shine), shared with Sanskrit candra (the moon, shining) and Welsh cann (white). Roman office-seekers wore a toga whitened with chalk — a candidatus is \"a man in white\" (candidate); candidus is pure white — frank (candid; candour is whiteness), candela a candle (chandelier is French), in-candescere to heat to white (incandescent), candor whiteness, brilliance; cinders is unrelated (Old English). Candida, the yeast, is \"white\"; Candide is Voltaire's innocent."],
  clam: ["clamare(喊)来自印欧语 *kelh₁-(喊),同根有 calare(召集——calendae 每月初一是祭司喊出来的日子,calendar 由此得名)、clarus(响亮)、希腊语 kalein、英语 low(牛叫)、hale/hail(呼唤)。罗马集会的 clamor 是喊声(clamour),ac-clamare 齐声喊——喝彩(acclaim),de-clamare 练习演说(declaim),ex-clamare 惊呼(exclaim),pro-clamare 公开喊——宣告(proclaim),re-clamare 喊回去——抗议(reclaim 后来转为\"收回\"),claim 是 clamare 经法语——先是\"喊着要\",后指主张;council 与此无关(concilium),但 nomenclature 是\"叫名\"。",
    "clamare (to shout) comes from PIE *kelh₁- (to call), shared with calare (to summon — the calendae, first of each month, were called out by the priests, whence calendar), clarus (loud), Greek kalein, English low (of cattle) and hail. The clamor of a Roman assembly is its clamour; ac-clamare to shout together — acclaim, de-clamare to practise speeches (declaim), ex-clamare to cry out (exclaim), pro-clamare to shout in public (proclaim), re-clamare to shout back — protest (reclaim later became \"take back\"); claim is clamare through French — first to cry out for, then to assert a right; nomenclature is name-calling."],
  not: ["nota(记号)与 noscere(认识)同根:印欧语 *ǵneh₃-(知道),同根有希腊语 gignōskein(gnosis)、英语 know、can(古义\"知道怎样\")、德语 kennen。nota 是罗马人在名单上做的记号——监察官在元老名字旁打 nota 就是谴责;notare 做记号(note、notation),notarius 是速记员(notary 后来指公证人),notitia 是被认识的状态——名声、通知(notice),notio 概念(notion),notus 已知的(notorious 是\"人尽皆知\"——先中性后贬义),de-notare 标示(denote),con-notare 一并标示(connote),an-notare 加注(annotate),nobilis 是 gnobilis——\"被人知道的\"——贵族(noble)。",
    "nota (a mark) shares a root with noscere (to come to know): PIE *ǵneh₃- (to know), shared with Greek gignōskein (gnosis), English know and can (once \"to know how\"), German kennen. A nota was the mark a Roman set against a name — the censor's nota beside a senator's name was a censure; notare to mark (note, notation), a notarius a shorthand clerk (later a notary), notitia the state of being known — fame, a notice, notio a concept (notion), notus known (notorious is \"known to all\" — neutral first, then bad), de-notare to mark out (denote), con-notare to mark along with (connote), an-notare to add notes (annotate), nobilis is gnobilis — \"known\" — noble."],
  prob: ["probus(好的、正直的)来自印欧语 *pro-bʰwo-(\"长在前面的\"——出色的),与 pro 和 fui(是)同根。probare 是\"判定为好\"——检验、证明:罗马监察官 probare 是验收公共工程;probatio 检验(probation 缓刑是\"考察期\"),probabilis 可以通过检验的——可能(probable),ap-probare 认可(approve),re-probare 否决(reprobate 被弃绝的人),im-probus 不正直,proof 与 prove 是 probare 经法语 prover/preuve,probe 探针是 proba(检验),probity 正直。\"例外证明规则\"里的 prove 本义是\"检验\"——例外检验规则。",
    "probus (good, upright) comes from PIE *pro-bʰwo- (growing forward — excellent), kin to pro and fui (I was). probare is to judge good — to test, to prove: a Roman censor probare a public work by inspecting it; probatio a test (probation is a trial period), probabilis able to pass the test — likely (probable), ap-probare to approve, re-probare to reject (a reprobate is one cast out), im-probus dishonest; proof and prove are probare through French prover/preuve, a probe is a proba, a testing rod, probity uprightness. In \"the exception proves the rule\", prove keeps its old sense: the exception tests the rule."],
  flor: ["flos(花,属格 floris)来自印欧语 *bʰleh₃-(开花),同根有英语 blossom、bloom、blow(古义开花)、德语 Blume、Blüte——拉丁语的 f 对应日耳曼语的 b。Flora 是罗马的花神,她的节日 Floralia(4 月底)是狂欢的春节;florere 开花——繁荣(flourish 经法语 florir),flos 的法语形状 fleur 给了 flower、flour(面粉是\"谷物的精华\"——两个词 18 世纪前同拼),Florence 是 Florentia\"开花之城\",florin 是佛罗伦萨 1252 年铸的百合花金币,fleur-de-lis 百合纹,florist 花商,florid 红润、华丽,de-florare 摘花,Flora 也指一地的植物总和(林奈 1745 年定的用法)。",
    "flos (a flower, genitive floris) comes from PIE *bʰleh₃- (to bloom), shared with English blossom, bloom and blow (once \"to flower\"), German Blume, Blüte — Latin f answers Germanic b. Flora was Rome's flower-goddess, and her Floralia at the end of April a riotous spring festival; florere is to flower — to flourish (through French florir), the French form fleur gave flower and flour (the \"flower\" or finest part of grain — one spelling until the 18th century), Florence is Florentia, the flowering city, the florin the gold lily-coin Florence struck in 1252, fleur-de-lis the lily device, a florist a flower-seller, florid ruddy or ornate, de-florare to pluck the flower; Flora also names a region's plants (Linnaeus, 1745)."],
  mens: ["metiri(量)的分词 mensus 与 mensis(月——最早的时间量度)、希腊语 metron(尺度,meter)、英语 measure(经法语)、moon、month 同根:印欧语 *meh₁-(量)。罗马的 mensura 是量度(measure),di-mensio 量出的各个方向(dimension),im-mensus 量不出的——无边(immense),mensa 与此无关(桌子);metron 给了 -meter(thermo-meter 1620 年代)、metre(1795 年法国定的长度)、geo-metry 量地、sym-metria 各部分同一尺度(symmetry),dia-meter 横量,peri-meter 绕着量,metronome 1815 年;commensurate 同尺度的。",
    "The participle mensus of metiri (to measure) shares a root with mensis (month — the first measure of time), Greek metron (a measure, meter), English measure (via French), moon and month: PIE *meh₁- (to measure). Roman mensura is a measure, di-mensio the measuring in each direction (dimension), im-mensus beyond measuring — immense; mensa, a table, is unrelated. metron gave -meter (thermo-meter in the 1620s), the metre (France's 1795 unit), geo-metry the measuring of land, sym-metria parts in one measure (symmetry), dia-meter measured across, peri-meter measured around, the metronome of 1815; commensurate means of the same measure."],
  par: ["par(相等的、成对的)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人说 par 是对手相当(par pari respondere 以牙还牙),也指一对;paritas 相等(parity),com-parare 放在一起比(compare;compere 是法语\"同伴\"),dis-par 不等(disparity、disparage 是把人配给不相称的婚姻——贬低),im-par 奇数,pair 是 par 经法语 paire(一对),peer 是 par 经法语 per——同等的人(贵族 peer 是彼此平等的人,peerless 无双),umpire 是 non-per(\"不成对的\"——第三方裁判,an oumpere 误分成 an umpire),par 在高尔夫是\"标准杆\"(1870 年),nonpareil 无与伦比。",
    "par (equal, matched) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Romans said par of well-matched opponents (par pari respondere, tit for tat) and of a pair; paritas equality (parity), com-parare to set side by side (compare; a compère is French for companion), dis-par unequal (disparity; disparage was marrying someone beneath their rank — belittling), im-par odd-numbered, pair is par through French paire, peer par through French per — an equal (peers of the realm are each other's equals, peerless without equal), umpire is non-per, the odd man — a third-party judge (an oumpere misdivided as an umpire), par in golf the standard score (1870), nonpareil without equal."],
  sum: ["sumere(拿、取)是 sub-emere(从下面拿起)的缩合——emere 本义\"拿\",后来专指\"买\"(印欧语 *h₁em-,同根有立陶宛语 imti 拿)。sumere 在复合词里最活跃:as-sumere 拿到自己身上——承担、假定(assume、assumption 也是圣母升天),con-sumere 拿光——消耗(consume、consumer),prae-sumere 事先拿定——推定(presume,presumptuous 是拿得太理所当然),re-sumere 拿回来——重新开始(resume;résumé 是法语\"摘要\"),sumptus 花费——sumptuous 奢华,sumptuary laws 限奢法。exempt 是 ex-emptus(拿出来的),redeem 是 red-imere(买回),premium 是 prae-emium(先拿到的——奖赏),example 是 ex-emplum(拿出来的样品),prompt 是 pro-emptus(拿到前面的——现成的)。",
    "sumere (to take) is sub-emere contracted, to take up from below — emere meant to take before it meant to buy (PIE *h₁em-, shared with Lithuanian imti, to take). sumere is busiest in compounds: as-sumere to take upon oneself — to assume (the Assumption is Mary taken up), con-sumere to take entirely — consume (consumer), prae-sumere to take beforehand — presume (presumptuous takes too much for granted), re-sumere to take back — resume (a résumé is French for summary), sumptus expense — sumptuous, sumptuary laws against luxury. Exempt is ex-emptus (taken out), redeem red-imere (to buy back), premium prae-emium (taken first — a reward), example ex-emplum (a sample taken out), prompt pro-emptus (taken forward — ready)."],
  tex: ["texere(织)来自印欧语 *teks-(编织、用斧砍削——制作),同根有希腊语 tektōn(木匠——architect 是\"首席工匠\")、technē(技艺——technique、technology)、梵语 takṣan(木匠)。罗马人的 textus 是织物,昆体良第一个把它比作文章的\"织理\"——text 文本由此;textura 织法(texture),textilis 织的(textile),con-textus 织在一起——上下文(context),prae-textus 织在前面的边——托词(pretext,托加袍的紫边叫 praetexta),sub-tilis 是 sub-tela\"经线下面的\"——细密、精妙(subtle),tissue 是 texere 的法语分词 tissu(织物,19 世纪转指生物组织),toile 帆布、toilet 是小块布——梳妆布。",
    "texere (to weave) comes from PIE *teks- (to weave, to hew — to fashion), shared with Greek tektōn (a builder — an architect is a chief builder), technē (craft — technique, technology), Sanskrit takṣan (carpenter). Roman textus was woven cloth; Quintilian first compared prose to a weave — whence text; textura the weaving (texture), textilis woven (textile), con-textus woven together — context, prae-textus the border woven in front — a pretext (the toga's purple border was the praetexta), sub-tilis is sub-tela, under the warp — finely woven, subtle; tissue is the French participle tissu, woven stuff (biological tissue only in the 19th century), toile canvas, toilet a little cloth — the dressing-cloth."],
  camp: ["campus(平野)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能与 campa(弯)无关而与希腊语 kampē 有关。罗马城外的 Campus Martius(战神广场)是公民操练、集会、投票的平地——所以 campus 从一开始就是\"公共活动的空地\";campania 是坎帕尼亚平原,法语 champagne 是\"平野\",香槟酒因产地得名;campaign 是军队\"下到平野\"作战的季节(冬天回营),19 世纪转指竞选;champion 是 campio——在 campus 上代人决斗的人(campus 也指决斗场),camp 是 campus 经法语\"驻地\",campanile 钟楼是坎帕尼亚人发明的,scamper 是 ex-campare 逃出战场,campus 指大学校园是 1774 年普林斯顿的用法。",
    "campus (a level field) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin. Outside Rome the Campus Martius, the Field of Mars, was the plain where citizens drilled, assembled and voted — so a campus was from the start open ground for public life; campania is the Campanian plain, French champagne \"open country\", the wine named from its region; a campaign was the season an army spent down in the field (winter was for quarters), extended to elections in the 19th century; a champion is a campio, one who fought in the campus on another's behalf (campus also meant the duelling ground), camp is campus through French, a campanile a Campanian bell-tower, to scamper is ex-campare, to bolt from the field, and campus for a college was Princeton usage from 1774."],
  chart: ["希腊语 khartēs(纸草纸)可能是埃及语借词。罗马人借为 charta——一张纸草纸,后来指任何书写材料和文书:Magna Carta 大宪章(1215),chartularium 契约集,carta 在意大利语里就是\"纸\",card(卡片、纸牌)是 charta 经法语 carte,chart 图表、海图是 16 世纪从 carte 分化,charter 特许状是小 charta(chartula),cartoon 是意大利语 cartone——画家的大幅草图纸,cartridge 是法语 cartouche(小纸筒,装火药),cartel 是意大利语 cartello(小纸条——挑战书,后指企业协议),carton 纸盒,à la carte 照单点菜,discard 是丢掉一张牌,cartography 制图。",
    "Greek khartēs (papyrus sheet) is probably an Egyptian loan. Rome borrowed it as charta — a sheet of papyrus, then any writing material or document: Magna Carta (1215), a chartularium a collection of deeds; Italian carta is simply paper; card is charta through French carte, chart split from carte in the 16th century for maps and tables, a charter is a little charta (chartula), a cartoon Italian cartone, the painter's large sketch-sheet, a cartridge French cartouche, a little paper tube of powder, a cartel Italian cartello, a little note — a challenge, later a business pact, a carton a paper box, à la carte from the card, to discard is to throw away a card, cartography map-drawing."],
  circ: ["circus(圆圈、赛马场)来自印欧语 *(s)ker-(弯、转),同根有希腊语 kirkos(圆环、猎鹰盘旋)、krikos(环)、英语 ring(经 *hringaz)、德语 Ring。罗马的 Circus Maximus 是二十五万人的赛车场;circa 大约(\"在周围\"),circum- 前缀(circum-ferre 带着绕——圆周 circumference,circum-stare 站在周围——circumstance,circum-navigare 环航),circulus 小圆(circle;circular 循环的,circulate 流通——哈维 1628 年用于血液),circuitus 绕一圈(circuit),search 是 circare(绕着找)经法语 cercher,cirque 法语马戏团,circus 现代马戏义是 1780 年代伦敦阿斯特利的圆形马场。",
    "circus (a ring, a racecourse) comes from PIE *(s)ker- (to bend, turn), shared with Greek kirkos (a ring, a wheeling hawk), krikos (ring), English ring (via *hringaz), German Ring. Rome's Circus Maximus seated a quarter of a million for the chariot races; circa is about (\"around\"), circum- the prefix (circum-ferre to carry round — circumference, circum-stare to stand around — circumstance, circum-navigare), circulus a little ring (circle; circular, circulate — Harvey used it of the blood in 1628), circuitus a going round (circuit), search is circare, to go round looking, through French cercher, cirque French for circus, and the modern circus began with Astley's circular riding-ring in London in the 1780s."],
  lim: ["limen(门槛)与 limes(界线、田埂)同根,词源不明——可能与 limus(斜的)有关。罗马人跨过 limen 进门,新娘被抱过门槛(不许绊到);limes 是罗马帝国的边墙(莱茵-多瑙河的 Limes Germanicus);e-liminare 是\"赶出门槛\"——消除(eliminate),prae-liminaris 门槛之前的(preliminary),sub-liminalis 门槛之下——阈下的(subliminal,1886 年心理学词),limitare 划界(limit、limitation),de-limitare 划定(delimit),limit 经法语 limite,sublime 是 sub-limen——\"到门楣之下\"——高耸、崇高(另一说法是\"斜着向上\"),lintel 门楣是 limitellus 经法语。",
    "limen (a threshold) and limes (a boundary path) share a root of uncertain origin — perhaps limus (oblique). Romans stepped over the limen into the house; a bride was carried over it so she would not stumble; the limes was the Empire's frontier wall (the Limes Germanicus along Rhine and Danube); e-liminare is to put out over the threshold — eliminate, prae-liminaris before the threshold (preliminary), sub-liminalis below the threshold (subliminal, a psychologist's word of 1886), limitare to bound (limit, limitation), de-limitare to mark out (delimit), sublime is sub-limen — up to the lintel — lofty (or, some say, \"rising obliquely\"), and lintel is limitellus through French."],
  lin: ["linum(亚麻)是地中海最古老的栽培植物之一,词可能是从某种前印欧语借入的,希腊语 linon、英语 linen、德语 Leinen、俄语 lën 同源。罗马人的 linea 是亚麻线——测量用的绳线,后来指任何\"线\":line 直线、行、血统(法语 lignée),linearis 线的(linear),de-lineare 勾线(delineate),linteum 麻布,lingerie 是法语的\"麻布衣\"(内衣),lining 衬里是 linen 做的,lint 麻絮,align 是排成一线(法语 aligner),outline 轮廓,linoleum 是 1863 年沃尔顿造的\"亚麻油布\",linseed 亚麻籽,crinoline 是马鬃(crin)加麻布的裙撑。",
    "linum (flax) is one of the Mediterranean's oldest crops, its name probably borrowed from a pre-Indo-European tongue; Greek linon, English linen, German Leinen, Russian lën are cognate. Roman linea was a flax thread — the surveyor's measuring line, then any line: a line drawn, a row, a lineage (French lignée), linearis linear, de-lineare to draw the line (delineate), linteum linen cloth, lingerie French for linen wear (underclothes), lining is linen inside a garment, lint flax fluff, align to set in a line (French aligner), outline, linoleum Walton's 1863 \"flax oil\" floorcloth, linseed flax seed, crinoline horsehair (crin) and linen stiffening."],
  plor: ["plorare(哭喊、哀号)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能是拟声。它自己没有传下来,但复合词很活:ex-plorare 本义\"喊出来\"——猎人喊叫把猎物赶出来,于是转为\"搜寻、侦察\"(explore、exploration 到 16 世纪才有\"探索\"义),de-plorare 大哭——痛惜(deplore),im-plorare 哭着求(implore)。法语 pleurer(哭)、西班牙语 llorar、意大利语 piangere 里前两个是 plorare 的后代。",
    "plorare (to cry out, wail) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin — perhaps imitative. It left no direct heir, but its compounds thrive: ex-plorare meant to cry out — hunters shouting to flush game — and so came to mean to search out, to scout (explore; exploration in the discovery sense dates from the 16th century), de-plorare to weep over — deplore, im-plorare to beg in tears — implore. French pleurer and Spanish llorar (to weep) are its descendants; Italian piangere is from plangere."],
  ops: ["希腊语 ops(眼、脸)与 optikos(视觉的)来自印欧语 *h₃ekʷ-(看、眼),同根有拉丁语 oculus、英语 eye、德语 Auge、梵语 akṣi。希腊语 optikē 是光学(optics,欧几里得写过《光学》),optician 配镜师,syn-opsis 一眼看全——概要(synopsis),aut-opsia 亲眼看——尸检,bi-opsy 活体看(1879 年),my-ops 是\"闭着眼\"——近视(myopia),Cyclops 是\"圆眼\"独眼巨人,Ethiops 是\"晒黑的脸\"(埃塞俄比亚),panopticon 是边沁 1791 年设计的\"全视\"监狱,autopsy、synoptic 福音书(前三卷可\"并观\")。",
    "Greek ops (eye, face) and optikos (of sight) come from PIE *h₃ekʷ- (to see; eye), shared with Latin oculus, English eye, German Auge, Sanskrit akṣi. Greek optikē is the science of sight (optics — Euclid wrote an Optics), an optician makes lenses, syn-opsis is seeing all at once — a summary, aut-opsia seeing for oneself — an autopsy, bi-opsy looking at living tissue (1879), my-ops \"shut-eyed\" — short-sighted (myopia), a Cyclops is round-eye, Ethiops the burnt face (Ethiopia), the panopticon Bentham's 1791 all-seeing prison, and the synoptic Gospels are the three that can be viewed together."],
  nav: ["navis(船)来自印欧语 *neh₂u-(船),同根有希腊语 naus(nautical、Argonaut)、梵语 nau、古英语 nowend(水手)、古挪威语 nór。罗马人 navigare 行船(navigate),navigatio 航行,navalis 船的(naval;navy 是 navia 经法语),nau-ta 水手(nautical 经希腊),naufragium 船破——海难,nausea 是希腊语 nautia——晕船(nausea 本义\"船病\"),nave 是教堂的中殿——像倒扣的船,nacelle 飞机的吊舱是小船,noise 竟也是 nausea 经法语的一种说法(有争议),navicula 小船,astronaut 星际水手(1929),cosmonaut。",
    "navis (a ship) comes from PIE *neh₂u- (boat), shared with Greek naus (nautical, Argonaut), Sanskrit nau, Old English nowend (sailor), Old Norse nór. Romans navigare to sail (navigate), navigatio a voyage, navalis of ships (naval; navy is navia through French), a nauta a sailor (nautical, via Greek), naufragium ship-breaking — a wreck, nausea Greek nautia — seasickness (nausea is literally ship-sickness), a church's nave is the great hall shaped like an upturned hull, a nacelle a little boat slung under an aircraft, noise is by one theory nausea through French, navicula a small boat, astronaut a star-sailor (1929), cosmonaut."],
});
